WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.479 align:middle line:84%
[SQUEAKING]
[RUSTLING] [CLICKING]

00:00:03.479 --> 00:00:15.828 align:middle line:90%


00:00:15.828 --> 00:00:18.120 align:middle line:84%
GLENN ELLISON: OK, I guess
I will start the tradition I

00:00:18.120 --> 00:00:20.250 align:middle line:84%
started last time
of starting at 9:04.

00:00:20.250 --> 00:00:23.013 align:middle line:84%
So if you remember last time,
I finished a lecture right

00:00:23.013 --> 00:00:24.180 align:middle line:90%
in the middle of an example.

00:00:24.180 --> 00:00:27.150 align:middle line:84%
I was talking about
monopoly information

00:00:27.150 --> 00:00:32.377 align:middle line:84%
design, which is about how
monopolists can affect--

00:00:32.377 --> 00:00:34.710 align:middle line:84%
by changing the information
that consumers receive about

00:00:34.710 --> 00:00:36.400 align:middle line:84%
their product can
affect the prices.

00:00:36.400 --> 00:00:39.813 align:middle line:84%
And this is motivation,
both for firms

00:00:39.813 --> 00:00:41.730 align:middle line:84%
who are giving information
about their product

00:00:41.730 --> 00:00:44.130 align:middle line:84%
to consumers to try
to maximize profits,

00:00:44.130 --> 00:00:47.610 align:middle line:84%
or potentially for platforms
like Amazon giving information

00:00:47.610 --> 00:00:50.905 align:middle line:84%
to consumers to try to
increase the consumer surplus

00:00:50.905 --> 00:00:53.280 align:middle line:84%
and limit the monopoly power
that the monopolist is going

00:00:53.280 --> 00:00:55.200 align:middle line:90%
to exert.

00:00:55.200 --> 00:00:57.960 align:middle line:84%
I had been multiplying
everything by 18

00:00:57.960 --> 00:01:00.880 align:middle line:84%
when I talked about the
examples last class in my head.

00:01:00.880 --> 00:01:05.880 align:middle line:84%
So I made this about the
Sharan sandwich at Clover

00:01:05.880 --> 00:01:08.550 align:middle line:84%
and said that if consumers
had full information

00:01:08.550 --> 00:01:11.820 align:middle line:84%
about the sandwich, their
values would be uniform.

00:01:11.820 --> 00:01:17.090 align:middle line:84%
On 0, 18, there's a cost of
$6 of producing the sandwich.

00:01:17.090 --> 00:01:18.970 align:middle line:84%
And I discussed
a few different--

00:01:18.970 --> 00:01:21.820 align:middle line:84%
how consumer surplus
and profit would

00:01:21.820 --> 00:01:24.520 align:middle line:84%
change a few different
information structures.

00:01:24.520 --> 00:01:26.410 align:middle line:84%
If you give the consumers
full information,

00:01:26.410 --> 00:01:28.930 align:middle line:84%
then their values
are uniform 0, 18.

00:01:28.930 --> 00:01:32.650 align:middle line:84%
When your cost is 6,
the monopoly profit

00:01:32.650 --> 00:01:36.640 align:middle line:84%
is halfway in between
6 and 18, which is 12.

00:01:36.640 --> 00:01:38.980 align:middle line:84%
Another example I
discussed is suppose Clover

00:01:38.980 --> 00:01:41.050 align:middle line:84%
puts the sandwich in a
bag and tells you nothing

00:01:41.050 --> 00:01:42.350 align:middle line:90%
other than it's a new sandwich.

00:01:42.350 --> 00:01:44.180 align:middle line:84%
We're not telling you
anything about it.

00:01:44.180 --> 00:01:47.890 align:middle line:84%
Then all consumers
would have values of 9.

00:01:47.890 --> 00:01:51.200 align:middle line:84%
And so the monopoly price
would be to set a price of 9.

00:01:51.200 --> 00:01:53.230 align:middle line:84%
And in this case, they
actually make more money,

00:01:53.230 --> 00:01:56.050 align:middle line:84%
but consumer surplus is
completely eliminated.

00:01:56.050 --> 00:01:59.202 align:middle line:84%
And then I gave this partial
information example, where

00:01:59.202 --> 00:02:01.660 align:middle line:84%
I gave the consumers partial
information about the product,

00:02:01.660 --> 00:02:03.890 align:middle line:84%
then ended up with a
monopoly price of 10.

00:02:03.890 --> 00:02:06.340 align:middle line:84%
And consumers and the
firm were both better off

00:02:06.340 --> 00:02:07.610 align:middle line:90%
than with full information.

00:02:07.610 --> 00:02:10.780 align:middle line:84%
And this is what that
example looked like.

00:02:10.780 --> 00:02:13.300 align:middle line:84%
I know people often ask
me, How on earth could

00:02:13.300 --> 00:02:14.600 align:middle line:90%
a firm do this in practice?

00:02:14.600 --> 00:02:16.725 align:middle line:84%
And let's imagine-- you
can think of this two ways.

00:02:16.725 --> 00:02:21.610 align:middle line:84%
One is, is this a model of an
omniscient firm like Amazon

00:02:21.610 --> 00:02:24.590 align:middle line:84%
or Google or Apple that
knows so much about you,

00:02:24.590 --> 00:02:26.140 align:middle line:84%
they know your
preferences better

00:02:26.140 --> 00:02:28.690 align:middle line:84%
than you do because they
know what people like

00:02:28.690 --> 00:02:29.830 align:middle line:90%
you thought of the product?

00:02:29.830 --> 00:02:32.330 align:middle line:84%
So they can actually tell you
about what your value is going

00:02:32.330 --> 00:02:34.420 align:middle line:90%
to be if you eat that sandwich.

00:02:34.420 --> 00:02:36.400 align:middle line:84%
The other example
could be, let's imagine

00:02:36.400 --> 00:02:39.280 align:middle line:84%
that the value for having a
sandwich depends on how much

00:02:39.280 --> 00:02:41.650 align:middle line:84%
you like cauliflower
and how much you

00:02:41.650 --> 00:02:44.630 align:middle line:84%
like the Indo-Chinese
sauce that it comes in.

00:02:44.630 --> 00:02:48.740 align:middle line:84%
And initially, you know
nothing about the sandwich.

00:02:48.740 --> 00:02:50.830 align:middle line:84%
But then one thing
they can tell you is,

00:02:50.830 --> 00:02:54.340 align:middle line:84%
I'm just telling you that the
sandwich is made of cauliflower

00:02:54.340 --> 00:02:55.490 align:middle line:90%
as the base.

00:02:55.490 --> 00:02:57.400 align:middle line:84%
And then people who
don't like cauliflower

00:02:57.400 --> 00:02:59.350 align:middle line:84%
are going to have
values between 0 and 6,

00:02:59.350 --> 00:03:01.180 align:middle line:84%
depending on how much
they like the sauce.

00:03:01.180 --> 00:03:03.340 align:middle line:84%
Those who have a medium
preference for cauliflower

00:03:03.340 --> 00:03:04.368 align:middle line:90%
would have these values.

00:03:04.368 --> 00:03:06.160 align:middle line:84%
Those who have a high
value for cauliflower

00:03:06.160 --> 00:03:07.070 align:middle line:90%
would have those values.

00:03:07.070 --> 00:03:09.010 align:middle line:84%
So it's like, we're not going to
tell you what the sauce is like

00:03:09.010 --> 00:03:11.410 align:middle line:84%
or the vegetables, but we'll
tell you that cauliflower

00:03:11.410 --> 00:03:13.270 align:middle line:90%
is the base of the sandwich.

00:03:13.270 --> 00:03:16.090 align:middle line:84%
Anyway, but what happened
with this example was

00:03:16.090 --> 00:03:18.190 align:middle line:84%
all the consumers, after
getting the signal,

00:03:18.190 --> 00:03:20.350 align:middle line:90%
had values of 3, 10, and 16.

00:03:20.350 --> 00:03:23.080 align:middle line:84%
And that ended up making
the monopoly price 10.

00:03:23.080 --> 00:03:26.800 align:middle line:84%
So anyway, the monopoly
information design literature

00:03:26.800 --> 00:03:29.510 align:middle line:84%
is about how can we
think more generally

00:03:29.510 --> 00:03:31.520 align:middle line:84%
about what types
of information we

00:03:31.520 --> 00:03:34.610 align:middle line:84%
want to give consumers to either
maximize profits or maximize

00:03:34.610 --> 00:03:38.250 align:middle line:84%
consumer surplus or do
something in between?

00:03:38.250 --> 00:03:43.260 align:middle line:84%
So the Roesler and Szentes paper
that I put on the reading list

00:03:43.260 --> 00:03:46.560 align:middle line:84%
assumes that the values are
now distributed according

00:03:46.560 --> 00:03:49.360 align:middle line:90%
to some distribution F on 0, 1.

00:03:49.360 --> 00:03:52.600 align:middle line:84%
It specializes
that the cost is 0.

00:03:52.600 --> 00:03:56.160 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to write mu for the
mean value of the consumers.

00:03:56.160 --> 00:03:58.860 align:middle line:84%
And what I'm going to
do is give this firm

00:03:58.860 --> 00:04:02.070 align:middle line:84%
tremendous ability to manipulate
the signal distribution.

00:04:02.070 --> 00:04:04.020 align:middle line:84%
What the firm can
do is choose signals

00:04:04.020 --> 00:04:07.980 align:middle line:84%
to choose any joint distribution
whatsoever on v and s.

00:04:07.980 --> 00:04:10.750 align:middle line:84%
So the most general set
of signal structures

00:04:10.750 --> 00:04:15.300 align:middle line:84%
is they're just some joint
distribution on v and s.

00:04:15.300 --> 00:04:17.935 align:middle line:84%
Notice that when
consumers get signal s,

00:04:17.935 --> 00:04:19.560 align:middle line:84%
what they're going
to be willing to pay

00:04:19.560 --> 00:04:21.779 align:middle line:90%
is expectation of v given s.

00:04:21.779 --> 00:04:25.410 align:middle line:84%
So rather than giving them a
signal with a label like low,

00:04:25.410 --> 00:04:28.980 align:middle line:84%
medium, high, I might
as well just say,

00:04:28.980 --> 00:04:31.950 align:middle line:84%
your signal is
either 3 or 10 or 16.

00:04:31.950 --> 00:04:35.470 align:middle line:90%


00:04:35.470 --> 00:04:37.720 align:middle line:84%
Given the information about
the product I'm revealing,

00:04:37.720 --> 00:04:40.660 align:middle line:84%
your expected value is 3,
your expected value is 10,

00:04:40.660 --> 00:04:42.520 align:middle line:90%
your expected value is 16.

00:04:42.520 --> 00:04:44.172 align:middle line:90%
And so you might as well--

00:04:44.172 --> 00:04:46.630 align:middle line:84%
consumers are going to do the
Bayesian updating of whatever

00:04:46.630 --> 00:04:47.950 align:middle line:90%
letters or words you give them.

00:04:47.950 --> 00:04:50.328 align:middle line:84%
So you might as well just do
the direct signal structure,

00:04:50.328 --> 00:04:52.120 align:middle line:84%
just tell them what
their expected value is

00:04:52.120 --> 00:04:54.520 align:middle line:90%
given what you've known.

00:04:54.520 --> 00:04:59.600 align:middle line:84%
Now, obviously, I can't pick any
distribution over values of s

00:04:59.600 --> 00:05:02.500 align:middle line:90%
I'm going to give them.

00:05:02.500 --> 00:05:05.470 align:middle line:84%
v is going to be s plus
epsilon, where expectation

00:05:05.470 --> 00:05:07.730 align:middle line:90%
of epsilon given s is 0.

00:05:07.730 --> 00:05:10.000 align:middle line:90%
And so that means two things.

00:05:10.000 --> 00:05:13.720 align:middle line:84%
One thing that it means is that
the expectation of the signals

00:05:13.720 --> 00:05:18.100 align:middle line:84%
must be mu because
the expectation of v

00:05:18.100 --> 00:05:23.140 align:middle line:84%
is expectation of s
plus epsilon given s--

00:05:23.140 --> 00:05:24.190 align:middle line:90%
iterated expectations.

00:05:24.190 --> 00:05:26.210 align:middle line:84%
I guess you must've
done [14.]380 already.

00:05:26.210 --> 00:05:29.038 align:middle line:84%
And so the expectation of
the signal distribution

00:05:29.038 --> 00:05:30.580 align:middle line:84%
has to be the same
as the expectation

00:05:30.580 --> 00:05:32.800 align:middle line:90%
of the prior distribution.

00:05:32.800 --> 00:05:34.690 align:middle line:84%
And the other thing
is that F is going

00:05:34.690 --> 00:05:39.640 align:middle line:84%
to be the distribution
of v, the true values,

00:05:39.640 --> 00:05:43.950 align:middle line:84%
is a mean-preserving spread
of the distribution of g.

00:05:43.950 --> 00:05:46.740 align:middle line:84%
And so what you want to think
about the monopolist is doing

00:05:46.740 --> 00:05:50.850 align:middle line:84%
is just choosing a distribution
g over possible signals,

00:05:50.850 --> 00:05:52.510 align:middle line:84%
subject to those
two constraints--

00:05:52.510 --> 00:05:57.300 align:middle line:84%
I've got to choose a
distribution that has mean mu,

00:05:57.300 --> 00:05:59.730 align:middle line:84%
and I have to choose a
distribution such that F is

00:05:59.730 --> 00:06:04.410 align:middle line:84%
a mean-preserving spread of
the distribution that I pick.

00:06:04.410 --> 00:06:07.440 align:middle line:84%
So, for instance, if this
is the true distribution,

00:06:07.440 --> 00:06:11.580 align:middle line:84%
I can't give signals like
this because this distribution

00:06:11.580 --> 00:06:14.640 align:middle line:84%
of finite support is not
a mean-preserving spread

00:06:14.640 --> 00:06:17.340 align:middle line:90%
of that bigger distribution.

00:06:17.340 --> 00:06:19.620 align:middle line:84%
I could choose
something like this

00:06:19.620 --> 00:06:22.910 align:middle line:84%
because this thing is a
mean-preserving spread of that.

00:06:22.910 --> 00:06:29.620 align:middle line:90%


00:06:29.620 --> 00:06:33.860 align:middle line:84%
So let me say, the
two problems here,

00:06:33.860 --> 00:06:35.920 align:middle line:84%
one is the profit
maximization problem.

00:06:35.920 --> 00:06:38.760 align:middle line:84%
The other is the consumer
surplus maximization problem.

00:06:38.760 --> 00:06:41.470 align:middle line:90%
Profit maximization is trivial.

00:06:41.470 --> 00:06:44.020 align:middle line:84%
It's economically
probably more important.

00:06:44.020 --> 00:06:45.810 align:middle line:84%
No one ever writes
about it because what

00:06:45.810 --> 00:06:47.470 align:middle line:90%
do you do to maximize profits?

00:06:47.470 --> 00:06:50.460 align:middle line:84%
You just tell consumers
your value is greater

00:06:50.460 --> 00:06:54.142 align:middle line:84%
than c or your value
is less than c.

00:06:54.142 --> 00:06:56.100 align:middle line:84%
And then if you tell them
your value is greater

00:06:56.100 --> 00:06:59.190 align:middle line:84%
than c, you sell it to them
at the price of p star,

00:06:59.190 --> 00:07:01.680 align:middle line:84%
which is just the
expectation of v,

00:07:01.680 --> 00:07:05.400 align:middle line:90%
given that v is greater than c.

00:07:05.400 --> 00:07:08.520 align:middle line:84%
So this simple pricing scheme,
just tell them your value

00:07:08.520 --> 00:07:11.730 align:middle line:84%
is above c or below
c, when you hear

00:07:11.730 --> 00:07:14.850 align:middle line:84%
your value is above
c, every consumer who

00:07:14.850 --> 00:07:17.560 align:middle line:84%
hears their value is above c is
willing to pay at this price.

00:07:17.560 --> 00:07:19.350 align:middle line:84%
So we get the fully
efficient outcome,

00:07:19.350 --> 00:07:22.980 align:middle line:84%
and we fully extract all
the consumer surplus.

00:07:22.980 --> 00:07:24.510 align:middle line:84%
So that's clearly
the best thing you

00:07:24.510 --> 00:07:26.593 align:middle line:84%
could do because you're
maximizing social surplus,

00:07:26.593 --> 00:07:29.490 align:middle line:84%
and the monopolist
gets all of it.

00:07:29.490 --> 00:07:31.815 align:middle line:84%
What do we do if,
instead, we're Amazon,

00:07:31.815 --> 00:07:35.268 align:middle line:84%
and we're trying to
maximize consumer surplus?

00:07:35.268 --> 00:07:36.810 align:middle line:84%
Intuitively, what
you're trying to do

00:07:36.810 --> 00:07:40.860 align:middle line:84%
is get the monopolist to
charge the lowest price it can

00:07:40.860 --> 00:07:43.230 align:middle line:84%
and sell to the largest number
of consumers that it can,

00:07:43.230 --> 00:07:46.320 align:middle line:84%
because that's the way you
generate consumer surplus is get

00:07:46.320 --> 00:07:47.985 align:middle line:84%
everyone with a
value greater than c

00:07:47.985 --> 00:07:50.855 align:middle line:84%
to buy and do it at the
lowest possible price.

00:07:50.855 --> 00:07:53.570 align:middle line:90%


00:07:53.570 --> 00:07:55.040 align:middle line:84%
Could potentially
be some trade-off

00:07:55.040 --> 00:07:56.130 align:middle line:90%
between those two objectives.

00:07:56.130 --> 00:07:57.110 align:middle line:90%
It turns out it's not.

00:07:57.110 --> 00:08:01.070 align:middle line:84%
In this problem, you want
to get everybody to buy.

00:08:01.070 --> 00:08:02.040 align:middle line:90%
So what do you do?

00:08:02.040 --> 00:08:03.385 align:middle line:90%
The answer is this.

00:08:03.385 --> 00:08:06.465 align:middle line:90%


00:08:06.465 --> 00:08:08.090 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to use some
distribution that

00:08:08.090 --> 00:08:09.270 align:middle line:90%
looks like that.

00:08:09.270 --> 00:08:13.130 align:middle line:84%
And so the two-step argument
to show that this is optimal

00:08:13.130 --> 00:08:16.700 align:middle line:84%
is I'm going to first look at
distributions of this form.

00:08:16.700 --> 00:08:19.157 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to show that
they work very well.

00:08:19.157 --> 00:08:20.990 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to show what
the maximum profit is

00:08:20.990 --> 00:08:22.980 align:middle line:84%
I can get with the
distribution of this form.

00:08:22.980 --> 00:08:25.355 align:middle line:84%
And then I'm going to show
that no other distribution can

00:08:25.355 --> 00:08:26.430 align:middle line:90%
do better than this one.

00:08:26.430 --> 00:08:30.690 align:middle line:84%
So it's like a guess and
check answer, I guess.

00:08:30.690 --> 00:08:33.419 align:middle line:84%
So what is that
distribution formally?

00:08:33.419 --> 00:08:35.600 align:middle line:90%
So I let the cdf--

00:08:35.600 --> 00:08:37.470 align:middle line:90%
I guess I graphed the pdf here.

00:08:37.470 --> 00:08:40.250 align:middle line:90%
So the cdf looks like this.

00:08:40.250 --> 00:08:46.760 align:middle line:84%
It starts at some value, s
lower bar, goes flat like that,

00:08:46.760 --> 00:08:55.950 align:middle line:84%
goes like that, and then has a
jump and then goes across at 1.

00:08:55.950 --> 00:08:59.770 align:middle line:90%
So this is s upper bar.

00:08:59.770 --> 00:09:04.750 align:middle line:90%
So this is my graph of G of s.

00:09:04.750 --> 00:09:07.077 align:middle line:84%
So this is the cumulative
distribution function

00:09:07.077 --> 00:09:08.535 align:middle line:84%
that I'm choosing
the signals from.

00:09:08.535 --> 00:09:14.270 align:middle line:90%


00:09:14.270 --> 00:09:19.760 align:middle line:84%
Notice that if the platform
chooses this distribution,

00:09:19.760 --> 00:09:27.410 align:middle line:84%
and the monopolist then picks
any price p in this interval,

00:09:27.410 --> 00:09:33.140 align:middle line:84%
the profit that the monopolist
gets is p times 1 minus G of p.

00:09:33.140 --> 00:09:39.110 align:middle line:84%
And p times 1 minus G of p is
just p times s lower bar over p

00:09:39.110 --> 00:09:41.420 align:middle line:90%
equals s lower bar.

00:09:41.420 --> 00:09:46.040 align:middle line:84%
So what this distribution does
is I choose that distribution,

00:09:46.040 --> 00:09:53.082 align:middle line:84%
and I get this graph of
s lower bar, s upper bar.

00:09:53.082 --> 00:09:54.540 align:middle line:84%
I get this distribution
of profits.

00:09:54.540 --> 00:09:59.900 align:middle line:84%
So profits are if I charge a
price of anything less than s

00:09:59.900 --> 00:10:01.880 align:middle line:90%
lower bar, everybody buys.

00:10:01.880 --> 00:10:06.330 align:middle line:84%
And so my profit is linear
in the price that I charge.

00:10:06.330 --> 00:10:08.630 align:middle line:84%
So I'm putting
price on this axis.

00:10:08.630 --> 00:10:12.950 align:middle line:84%
Then here, everybody buys,
and I get a price of--

00:10:12.950 --> 00:10:15.210 align:middle line:84%
I get a profit of
exactly s lower bar.

00:10:15.210 --> 00:10:16.520 align:middle line:90%
And then I've made people--

00:10:16.520 --> 00:10:21.150 align:middle line:84%
I've made you indifferent
over all prices in this range.

00:10:21.150 --> 00:10:23.220 align:middle line:90%
And then my profit drops to 0.

00:10:23.220 --> 00:10:26.250 align:middle line:84%
So this, I'm graphing the
profit that you get charging p.

00:10:26.250 --> 00:10:31.430 align:middle line:90%


00:10:31.430 --> 00:10:36.650 align:middle line:84%
So given that the monopolist
is indifferent over all prices

00:10:36.650 --> 00:10:42.060 align:middle line:84%
in this interval, one of the
optimal prices is s lower bar.

00:10:42.060 --> 00:10:44.700 align:middle line:84%
And so by setting this
distribution, and then

00:10:44.700 --> 00:10:46.650 align:middle line:84%
the monopolist is
indifferent over all prices,

00:10:46.650 --> 00:10:48.890 align:middle line:90%
it can charge price s lower bar.

00:10:48.890 --> 00:10:52.920 align:middle line:84%
We observe we get
profit s lower bar.

00:10:52.920 --> 00:10:56.940 align:middle line:84%
And then the consumer
surplus is the difference

00:10:56.940 --> 00:11:00.210 align:middle line:84%
between each of these
values and s lower bar.

00:11:00.210 --> 00:11:03.720 align:middle line:84%
So if you integrate
under this distribution,

00:11:03.720 --> 00:11:05.940 align:middle line:84%
you're going to get what
the consumer surplus is.

00:11:05.940 --> 00:11:08.220 align:middle line:84%
And so by putting s
lower bar down here,

00:11:08.220 --> 00:11:11.350 align:middle line:84%
the lower I can put it, the more
consumer surplus I'm generating.

00:11:11.350 --> 00:11:14.862 align:middle line:90%


00:11:14.862 --> 00:11:18.190 align:middle line:84%
So within this class of G's,
the consumer surplus optimal

00:11:18.190 --> 00:11:18.950 align:middle line:90%
solution is clear.

00:11:18.950 --> 00:11:20.617 align:middle line:84%
What I want to do is
I just want to pick

00:11:20.617 --> 00:11:24.610 align:middle line:84%
the smallest s lower bar that
I can subject to the constraint

00:11:24.610 --> 00:11:30.190 align:middle line:84%
that G has to have
mean mu and F has

00:11:30.190 --> 00:11:35.610 align:middle line:84%
to be a mean-preserving
spread of G. How low

00:11:35.610 --> 00:11:37.380 align:middle line:90%
can I go and still do that?

00:11:37.380 --> 00:11:41.190 align:middle line:84%
Well, this distribution has the
property that because it's only

00:11:41.190 --> 00:11:46.020 align:middle line:84%
going down like 1/s, if I were
to set s upper bar going towards

00:11:46.020 --> 00:11:49.470 align:middle line:84%
infinity, the mean of
this distribution would go

00:11:49.470 --> 00:11:50.950 align:middle line:90%
to infinity.

00:11:50.950 --> 00:11:54.760 align:middle line:84%
So for any s lower bar, I can
just pick a high enough s lower

00:11:54.760 --> 00:11:57.240 align:middle line:90%
bar and make the mean mu.

00:11:57.240 --> 00:12:03.240 align:middle line:84%
But the problem I run into is
I need this distribution to be

00:12:03.240 --> 00:12:06.998 align:middle line:84%
a mean-preserving
spread of F. You guys--

00:12:06.998 --> 00:12:07.540 align:middle line:90%
I don't know.

00:12:07.540 --> 00:12:09.248 align:middle line:84%
Do they still even do
this in statistics?

00:12:09.248 --> 00:12:11.840 align:middle line:84%
What does a mean-preserving
spread look like?

00:12:11.840 --> 00:12:16.160 align:middle line:84%
So If this is G, and this is F,
G is a mean-preserving spread

00:12:16.160 --> 00:12:19.070 align:middle line:84%
of F. And it's a
mean-preserving spread of F

00:12:19.070 --> 00:12:24.200 align:middle line:84%
because it crosses the
cdf of F once from below.

00:12:24.200 --> 00:12:26.570 align:middle line:84%
If F looked like
this, this thing

00:12:26.570 --> 00:12:30.570 align:middle line:90%
would not be valid because--

00:12:30.570 --> 00:12:33.910 align:middle line:90%
oh, no, that would still be OK.

00:12:33.910 --> 00:12:36.580 align:middle line:84%
The problem is going to
come in if let's suppose

00:12:36.580 --> 00:12:45.390 align:middle line:84%
that F were to go like
this, then it never crosses.

00:12:45.390 --> 00:12:48.020 align:middle line:84%
And so if I'm having to
use an s upper bar that's

00:12:48.020 --> 00:12:50.840 align:middle line:84%
bigger than the upper bound
of the F distribution,

00:12:50.840 --> 00:12:53.090 align:middle line:84%
then this thing isn't a
mean-preserving spread

00:12:53.090 --> 00:12:55.040 align:middle line:90%
of F anymore.

00:12:55.040 --> 00:13:00.230 align:middle line:84%
And so what I'm going to want to
do is just pick an s lower bar

00:13:00.230 --> 00:13:02.270 align:middle line:84%
and pick the smallest
s lower bar such

00:13:02.270 --> 00:13:07.820 align:middle line:84%
that when I put the upper
bound below the upper bound 1,

00:13:07.820 --> 00:13:09.670 align:middle line:90%
I'm getting a mean of mu.

00:13:09.670 --> 00:13:13.440 align:middle line:90%


00:13:13.440 --> 00:13:15.430 align:middle line:84%
So anyway, so that's
the best we can

00:13:15.430 --> 00:13:18.700 align:middle line:84%
do within that class
of distributions.

00:13:18.700 --> 00:13:21.407 align:middle line:84%
And then step 2, the
harder step of the proof--

00:13:21.407 --> 00:13:23.740 align:middle line:84%
actually, I think I've done
it pretty well on the slides

00:13:23.740 --> 00:13:24.860 align:middle line:84%
if you want to look
at the slides at home.

00:13:24.860 --> 00:13:26.470 align:middle line:84%
But the basic idea
in the slides is

00:13:26.470 --> 00:13:29.050 align:middle line:84%
that no other distribution
could possibly

00:13:29.050 --> 00:13:31.330 align:middle line:90%
do better than this one.

00:13:31.330 --> 00:13:33.710 align:middle line:90%
And how does that argument go?

00:13:33.710 --> 00:13:36.550 align:middle line:84%
Well, what I'm going to
do is I'm going to say,

00:13:36.550 --> 00:13:40.380 align:middle line:90%
let G be any other valid choice.

00:13:40.380 --> 00:13:43.740 align:middle line:84%
If you give me G
is a valid choice,

00:13:43.740 --> 00:13:46.320 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to pick a
distribution in this class

00:13:46.320 --> 00:13:50.460 align:middle line:84%
and show that it does at least
as well as G and is valid.

00:13:50.460 --> 00:13:56.700 align:middle line:84%
So you give me any other
valid distribution,

00:13:56.700 --> 00:14:01.620 align:middle line:84%
let pi be the profit that
you earn when you use G,

00:14:01.620 --> 00:14:04.230 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to consider
the distribution G of pi

00:14:04.230 --> 00:14:07.920 align:middle line:84%
s upper bar where s
upper bar is chosen such

00:14:07.920 --> 00:14:10.415 align:middle line:84%
that s upper bar gives
this thing a mean of mu.

00:14:10.415 --> 00:14:14.310 align:middle line:90%


00:14:14.310 --> 00:14:17.760 align:middle line:84%
This distribution,
G of pi s upper bar,

00:14:17.760 --> 00:14:21.090 align:middle line:84%
it also gives-- it gives
the same profit as in G,

00:14:21.090 --> 00:14:24.120 align:middle line:84%
because these distributions
G of s lower bar s upper bar

00:14:24.120 --> 00:14:25.840 align:middle line:90%
give profit s lower bar.

00:14:25.840 --> 00:14:28.800 align:middle line:84%
So if I choose the profit
I get from using this,

00:14:28.800 --> 00:14:32.110 align:middle line:84%
this thing also gives
the same profit pi.

00:14:32.110 --> 00:14:34.570 align:middle line:90%
It gives the same profit pi.

00:14:34.570 --> 00:14:36.410 align:middle line:90%
it's fully socially efficient.

00:14:36.410 --> 00:14:39.010 align:middle line:84%
Everybody purchases
in that distribution,

00:14:39.010 --> 00:14:42.100 align:middle line:84%
so it has to give at least
as much social surplus.

00:14:42.100 --> 00:14:45.950 align:middle line:84%
Because it's maximizing social
welfare, it has the same profit,

00:14:45.950 --> 00:14:48.530 align:middle line:84%
consumer surplus has
to be at least as high.

00:14:48.530 --> 00:14:53.390 align:middle line:84%
So if I switch from G
to G of pi s upper bar,

00:14:53.390 --> 00:14:56.960 align:middle line:84%
then I've gotten at least
as much consumer surplus

00:14:56.960 --> 00:14:59.580 align:middle line:90%
to complete the proof.

00:14:59.580 --> 00:15:01.730 align:middle line:84%
I just then need to
show that G of pi

00:15:01.730 --> 00:15:05.780 align:middle line:84%
s upper bar is a valid
distribution to choose.

00:15:05.780 --> 00:15:07.670 align:middle line:84%
And to do that, I
just need to show

00:15:07.670 --> 00:15:15.300 align:middle line:84%
that F is a mean-preserving
spread of G of pi s.

00:15:15.300 --> 00:15:16.860 align:middle line:90%
To do that--

00:15:16.860 --> 00:15:25.770 align:middle line:84%
I'm trying to show that F is a
mean-preserving spread of G pi s

00:15:25.770 --> 00:15:26.690 align:middle line:90%
upper bar.

00:15:26.690 --> 00:15:28.270 align:middle line:90%
I do that in two steps.

00:15:28.270 --> 00:15:36.180 align:middle line:84%
I show that-- we know that F is
a mean-preserving spread of G

00:15:36.180 --> 00:15:37.750 align:middle line:90%
because G was valid.

00:15:37.750 --> 00:15:40.620 align:middle line:84%
And so I just need
to show that any G is

00:15:40.620 --> 00:15:45.450 align:middle line:84%
a mean-preserving spread
of G of pi s upper bar.

00:15:45.450 --> 00:15:47.010 align:middle line:84%
And the way I'm
going to do that is

00:15:47.010 --> 00:15:51.230 align:middle line:90%
show that any G looks like this.

00:15:51.230 --> 00:16:00.510 align:middle line:84%
Any G that's valid has to start
above G of pi s upper bar here--

00:16:00.510 --> 00:16:07.800 align:middle line:84%
has to start above it, and
then it has to end below it.

00:16:07.800 --> 00:16:10.030 align:middle line:84%
Anyway, I've got
the argument here.

00:16:10.030 --> 00:16:12.660 align:middle line:84%
But basically, the argument
is it has to be above it here

00:16:12.660 --> 00:16:14.370 align:middle line:90%
because this one is 0.

00:16:14.370 --> 00:16:17.640 align:middle line:84%
In this interval, it
has to be above it here

00:16:17.640 --> 00:16:21.390 align:middle line:84%
because this one, the maximized
profit, is at most pi.

00:16:21.390 --> 00:16:24.140 align:middle line:84%
This one, the maximized
profit is exactly equal to pi.

00:16:24.140 --> 00:16:26.260 align:middle line:84%
So you have to sell
to fewer consumers.

00:16:26.260 --> 00:16:28.080 align:middle line:84%
So it's above it
all the way to here,

00:16:28.080 --> 00:16:30.760 align:middle line:84%
and then it has to be below
it here because that one's 1.

00:16:30.760 --> 00:16:32.320 align:middle line:90%
That's the basic argument.

00:16:32.320 --> 00:16:38.080 align:middle line:84%
So it's a-- what
are my takeaways--

00:16:38.080 --> 00:16:41.250 align:middle line:90%
let me get my takeaways first.

00:16:41.250 --> 00:16:44.410 align:middle line:84%
Takeaways, profit-maximizing
structure is simple.

00:16:44.410 --> 00:16:47.557 align:middle line:84%
You just tell people that
their value is at least c.

00:16:47.557 --> 00:16:49.390 align:middle line:84%
A good way to think
about information design

00:16:49.390 --> 00:16:51.250 align:middle line:84%
is think about choosing the
distribution of the consumer's

00:16:51.250 --> 00:16:52.210 align:middle line:90%
posterior.

00:16:52.210 --> 00:16:56.480 align:middle line:90%
And if you want to do that--

00:16:56.480 --> 00:16:59.098 align:middle line:90%
if you want to minimize--

00:16:59.098 --> 00:17:01.640 align:middle line:84%
if you want to maximize consumer
surplus, what you want to do

00:17:01.640 --> 00:17:03.350 align:middle line:84%
is get everyone to
buy, and get everyone

00:17:03.350 --> 00:17:05.609 align:middle line:84%
to buy at the lowest
price possible.

00:17:05.609 --> 00:17:08.930 align:middle line:84%
The way you get people to buy
at the lowest price possible

00:17:08.930 --> 00:17:13.099 align:middle line:84%
is to use a distribution
like this where there's

00:17:13.099 --> 00:17:15.089 align:middle line:90%
a lot of low-value consumers.

00:17:15.089 --> 00:17:18.890 align:middle line:84%
And if you raise your price at
all, you lose a lot of demand.

00:17:18.890 --> 00:17:22.685 align:middle line:84%
And demand is very steep here,
so that gets the monopolist

00:17:22.685 --> 00:17:24.560 align:middle line:84%
to be indifferent between
charging this price

00:17:24.560 --> 00:17:26.371 align:middle line:84%
and charging those
higher prices.

00:17:26.371 --> 00:17:27.829 align:middle line:84%
And so that's the
kind of thing you

00:17:27.829 --> 00:17:29.840 align:middle line:84%
want to do with the
information structure is

00:17:29.840 --> 00:17:32.230 align:middle line:84%
create a lot of
low-value consumers

00:17:32.230 --> 00:17:36.080 align:middle line:84%
and create a distribution
where every time the monopolist

00:17:36.080 --> 00:17:37.940 align:middle line:84%
raises their price
a little bit, they

00:17:37.940 --> 00:17:39.800 align:middle line:84%
would be losing lots
and lots of consumers.

00:17:39.800 --> 00:17:41.450 align:middle line:84%
And distributions
like this are the way

00:17:41.450 --> 00:17:43.480 align:middle line:90%
you maximize consumer surplus.

00:17:43.480 --> 00:17:47.072 align:middle line:90%


00:17:47.072 --> 00:17:51.610 align:middle line:84%
Also, Paolo had actually asked
after the last class, What's

00:17:51.610 --> 00:17:54.650 align:middle line:84%
the full set of things we can
do, and how do I show that?

00:17:54.650 --> 00:17:58.218 align:middle line:84%
I added a slide that
I won't talk about.

00:17:58.218 --> 00:18:00.760 align:middle line:84%
But Roesler and Szentes just
have this fairly simple argument

00:18:00.760 --> 00:18:05.110 align:middle line:84%
that shows that this is the
profit-maximizing point.

00:18:05.110 --> 00:18:08.090 align:middle line:84%
This is the consumer
surplus maximizing point.

00:18:08.090 --> 00:18:10.850 align:middle line:84%
Everything in this
triangle is possible.

00:18:10.850 --> 00:18:12.970 align:middle line:84%
So if you want to
achieve less efficiency,

00:18:12.970 --> 00:18:14.665 align:middle line:90%
you can achieve less efficiency.

00:18:14.665 --> 00:18:16.540 align:middle line:84%
But basically, what you
can do is give them--

00:18:16.540 --> 00:18:19.010 align:middle line:84%
you have to give a monopolist
at least this much profit.

00:18:19.010 --> 00:18:20.170 align:middle line:84%
If you were trying to
hurt the monopolist,

00:18:20.170 --> 00:18:21.310 align:middle line:90%
you can't do any better.

00:18:21.310 --> 00:18:23.118 align:middle line:84%
And consumer
surplus-- anyway, it's

00:18:23.118 --> 00:18:24.660 align:middle line:84%
going to have to be
in that triangle.

00:18:24.660 --> 00:18:27.580 align:middle line:90%


00:18:27.580 --> 00:18:34.290 align:middle line:84%
I really think it's probably
mostly this diagonal

00:18:34.290 --> 00:18:36.660 align:middle line:84%
that we're typically interested
in, how do we maximize

00:18:36.660 --> 00:18:38.680 align:middle line:90%
consumer surplus versus profit.

00:18:38.680 --> 00:18:41.310 align:middle line:84%
And the way we do this is we
choose these distributions

00:18:41.310 --> 00:18:43.230 align:middle line:90%
G of s lower bar, s upper bar.

00:18:43.230 --> 00:18:46.680 align:middle line:90%
And we just vary s lower bar.

00:18:46.680 --> 00:18:50.130 align:middle line:84%
And the higher we
make s lower bar,

00:18:50.130 --> 00:18:52.260 align:middle line:84%
the higher is the
monopolist's profit,

00:18:52.260 --> 00:18:54.240 align:middle line:90%
the lower is consumer surplus.

00:18:54.240 --> 00:18:56.550 align:middle line:84%
In some sense, this
is a special case

00:18:56.550 --> 00:18:59.250 align:middle line:84%
of this argument
where you just set s

00:18:59.250 --> 00:19:01.980 align:middle line:84%
lower bar equals
expectation of v,

00:19:01.980 --> 00:19:04.320 align:middle line:90%
given that v is greater than c.

00:19:04.320 --> 00:19:06.430 align:middle line:90%
That's the monopoly solution.

00:19:06.430 --> 00:19:08.170 align:middle line:84%
That's the monopoly
pricing solution.

00:19:08.170 --> 00:19:15.060 align:middle line:84%
And so as we raise s
upper bar to that point,

00:19:15.060 --> 00:19:16.990 align:middle line:90%
that's what we're going to do.

00:19:16.990 --> 00:19:20.960 align:middle line:90%


00:19:20.960 --> 00:19:22.216 align:middle line:90%
Any questions?

00:19:22.216 --> 00:19:26.750 align:middle line:90%


00:19:26.750 --> 00:19:29.030 align:middle line:90%
Let me say, I feel this is--

00:19:29.030 --> 00:19:31.990 align:middle line:84%
just in terms of thinking about
topics, I don't really know.

00:19:31.990 --> 00:19:33.140 align:middle line:90%
I looked.

00:19:33.140 --> 00:19:35.540 align:middle line:84%
I couldn't find any
good empirical papers

00:19:35.540 --> 00:19:36.960 align:middle line:90%
on monopoly information design.

00:19:36.960 --> 00:19:39.293 align:middle line:84%
It seems like that's something
that I think there should

00:19:39.293 --> 00:19:40.740 align:middle line:90%
be some empirical work on.

00:19:40.740 --> 00:19:44.810 align:middle line:84%
And also, this question of
translating this from theory

00:19:44.810 --> 00:19:48.950 align:middle line:84%
to IO is this "I can choose any
joint distribution over v and s

00:19:48.950 --> 00:19:52.920 align:middle line:84%
that's possible" is
a heroic assumption.

00:19:52.920 --> 00:19:54.590 align:middle line:90%
And I guess the question--

00:19:54.590 --> 00:19:56.600 align:middle line:84%
intriguing empirical
question is,

00:19:56.600 --> 00:19:59.210 align:middle line:84%
how can one do that in
practice, and how close can one

00:19:59.210 --> 00:20:04.010 align:middle line:84%
get to designing expectation
of the distributions

00:20:04.010 --> 00:20:07.190 align:middle line:84%
over expectation of
v, given the signals,

00:20:07.190 --> 00:20:10.520 align:middle line:84%
with a finite set of signals
or signals about product

00:20:10.520 --> 00:20:13.490 align:middle line:84%
characteristics or a
more limited set of ways

00:20:13.490 --> 00:20:15.680 align:middle line:84%
I can give signals than
just this telling people

00:20:15.680 --> 00:20:17.340 align:middle line:90%
their posteriors immediately?

00:20:17.340 --> 00:20:20.290 align:middle line:90%


00:20:20.290 --> 00:20:22.360 align:middle line:84%
Anyway, so rest
of today's class,

00:20:22.360 --> 00:20:24.580 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to start
doing empirical papers

00:20:24.580 --> 00:20:27.370 align:middle line:90%
for the first time.

00:20:27.370 --> 00:20:31.010 align:middle line:84%
And I'm going to mostly
talk about two papers.

00:20:31.010 --> 00:20:33.410 align:middle line:84%
What I thought I would
do first, though,

00:20:33.410 --> 00:20:37.370 align:middle line:84%
is go back and give you the
8-minute history of empirical

00:20:37.370 --> 00:20:38.540 align:middle line:90%
IO.

00:20:38.540 --> 00:20:41.540 align:middle line:84%
I mean, I think the background
is useful to understand.

00:20:41.540 --> 00:20:43.250 align:middle line:84%
As you start reading
IO papers, there

00:20:43.250 --> 00:20:45.625 align:middle line:84%
are a lot of IO papers written
about ketchup or cardboard

00:20:45.625 --> 00:20:48.350 align:middle line:84%
boxes, and people often
ask me why on earth

00:20:48.350 --> 00:20:49.770 align:middle line:90%
do IO economists do that.

00:20:49.770 --> 00:20:53.360 align:middle line:84%
And so I think giving some
perspective on what IO people do

00:20:53.360 --> 00:20:57.440 align:middle line:90%
and why could be useful.

00:20:57.440 --> 00:20:59.510 align:middle line:84%
Let me start my brief
empirical history.

00:20:59.510 --> 00:21:02.510 align:middle line:90%
So early empirical IO--

00:21:02.510 --> 00:21:07.670 align:middle line:84%
so before the 1960s, IO
economists wrote books.

00:21:07.670 --> 00:21:09.330 align:middle line:90%
And they wrote big, thick books.

00:21:09.330 --> 00:21:10.940 align:middle line:84%
They wrote books--
an IO economist was

00:21:10.940 --> 00:21:15.380 align:middle line:84%
an expert in some industry like
the airline industry or the car

00:21:15.380 --> 00:21:19.080 align:middle line:84%
industry or the coal industry or
the steel industry or whatever.

00:21:19.080 --> 00:21:21.630 align:middle line:84%
But IO economists were
experts in industries.

00:21:21.630 --> 00:21:26.060 align:middle line:84%
They wrote these big,
thick books discussing lots

00:21:26.060 --> 00:21:28.690 align:middle line:90%
of stuff about the industry.

00:21:28.690 --> 00:21:31.190 align:middle line:84%
We actually used to have an
assignment in this course, which

00:21:31.190 --> 00:21:33.950 align:middle line:84%
was try to get a
Harvard library card,

00:21:33.950 --> 00:21:36.290 align:middle line:84%
go into the stacks
of Widener, pick out

00:21:36.290 --> 00:21:39.807 align:middle line:84%
one of these many books,
read it, and write something

00:21:39.807 --> 00:21:41.390 align:middle line:84%
about it, because
it was often they're

00:21:41.390 --> 00:21:43.820 align:middle line:84%
full of facts and full of
interesting things going on

00:21:43.820 --> 00:21:44.840 align:middle line:90%
in the industry.

00:21:44.840 --> 00:21:47.960 align:middle line:84%
And they often have lots of
puzzles about what's going on

00:21:47.960 --> 00:21:50.810 align:middle line:84%
and why is this happening when,
with modern theory, you're like,

00:21:50.810 --> 00:21:52.580 align:middle line:84%
OK, I could tell you
why that's happening,

00:21:52.580 --> 00:21:56.640 align:middle line:84%
or this is a good example
of search affecting pricing

00:21:56.640 --> 00:22:02.100 align:middle line:84%
or whatever, some kind of
incomplete information story.

00:22:02.100 --> 00:22:04.200 align:middle line:84%
It was a very interesting
field, in the sense

00:22:04.200 --> 00:22:05.790 align:middle line:84%
that you would read books
about these industries,

00:22:05.790 --> 00:22:07.990 align:middle line:84%
and you would learn a
tremendous amount about them.

00:22:07.990 --> 00:22:10.390 align:middle line:84%
They were typically
very light on theory.

00:22:10.390 --> 00:22:12.538 align:middle line:84%
It was really a lot of
descriptive work about how

00:22:12.538 --> 00:22:14.580 align:middle line:84%
does this industry work,
what are the firms, what

00:22:14.580 --> 00:22:17.062 align:middle line:84%
are the prices like, what
are the markups, what

00:22:17.062 --> 00:22:18.770 align:middle line:84%
are all the practices
that you see there.

00:22:18.770 --> 00:22:22.590 align:middle line:90%


00:22:22.590 --> 00:22:29.880 align:middle line:84%
So in the '60s and '70s,
there was this increasing use

00:22:29.880 --> 00:22:31.890 align:middle line:90%
of statistics in economics.

00:22:31.890 --> 00:22:36.810 align:middle line:84%
And instead of just writing
discussions of industries,

00:22:36.810 --> 00:22:39.690 align:middle line:84%
people wanted to have
regressions in their papers.

00:22:39.690 --> 00:22:42.120 align:middle line:84%
And so regression started
to come into the profession

00:22:42.120 --> 00:22:43.530 align:middle line:90%
in the 1950s.

00:22:43.530 --> 00:22:47.068 align:middle line:84%
I have this one anecdote here
that my wife got from Zvi

00:22:47.068 --> 00:22:49.110 align:middle line:84%
Griliches, who was a famous
economist at Harvard,

00:22:49.110 --> 00:22:52.620 align:middle line:84%
that his summer job at some
point in the 1950s was to run

00:22:52.620 --> 00:22:53.230 align:middle line:90%
a regression.

00:22:53.230 --> 00:22:55.230 align:middle line:84%
And you might ask, well,
OK, was your summer job

00:22:55.230 --> 00:22:55.980 align:middle line:90%
to run regression?

00:22:55.980 --> 00:22:58.050 align:middle line:84%
It's like, no, he was
given the data set,

00:22:58.050 --> 00:23:02.770 align:middle line:84%
and his job was to compute x
prime x and then invert it.

00:23:02.770 --> 00:23:06.420 align:middle line:84%
And it was, like, a 7-variable
regression or something like

00:23:06.420 --> 00:23:06.920 align:middle line:90%
that.

00:23:06.920 --> 00:23:09.550 align:middle line:84%
So his summer job was with
a calculator, like, here's

00:23:09.550 --> 00:23:11.200 align:middle line:90%
a 7 by 7 matrix.

00:23:11.200 --> 00:23:15.220 align:middle line:84%
I want you to invert x prime
x so that we can then report

00:23:15.220 --> 00:23:17.860 align:middle line:90%
the results of that regression.

00:23:17.860 --> 00:23:22.480 align:middle line:84%
But anyway, obviously, this got
easier in the '50s and '60s when

00:23:22.480 --> 00:23:25.870 align:middle line:90%
computers got invented.

00:23:25.870 --> 00:23:29.770 align:middle line:84%
And Joe Bain, who was
actually a first one

00:23:29.770 --> 00:23:33.190 align:middle line:84%
to do regressions in a
within-industry study,

00:23:33.190 --> 00:23:36.040 align:middle line:84%
had a view that IO
shouldn't be developing

00:23:36.040 --> 00:23:38.750 align:middle line:84%
these facts about the coal
industry or the steel industry.

00:23:38.750 --> 00:23:41.720 align:middle line:84%
We want broad truths that
apply to all industries.

00:23:41.720 --> 00:23:43.000 align:middle line:90%
And so it became popular.

00:23:43.000 --> 00:23:46.130 align:middle line:84%
And IO after that was
cross-section regressions.

00:23:46.130 --> 00:23:48.460 align:middle line:84%
And what happened in these
cross-section regressions

00:23:48.460 --> 00:23:50.590 align:middle line:84%
was people typically
have a dependent variable

00:23:50.590 --> 00:23:54.610 align:middle line:90%
like markups in industry i.

00:23:54.610 --> 00:23:57.460 align:middle line:84%
And they would regress
markup on industry i.

00:23:57.460 --> 00:24:02.230 align:middle line:84%
So it would be like
markup in industry i

00:24:02.230 --> 00:24:08.950 align:middle line:84%
is alpha 0 plus alpha 1 times
the four-firm concentration

00:24:08.950 --> 00:24:22.490 align:middle line:84%
ratio plus alpha 2 times the
advertising to sales ratio,

00:24:22.490 --> 00:24:24.890 align:middle line:84%
and there'd be a bunch
of other variables.

00:24:24.890 --> 00:24:26.900 align:middle line:90%
But you would just do--

00:24:26.900 --> 00:24:30.140 align:middle line:84%
I'm running a regression like
this with six or seven or eight

00:24:30.140 --> 00:24:32.810 align:middle line:84%
variables, and I'm going to try
to talk about what is it that

00:24:32.810 --> 00:24:35.660 align:middle line:90%
makes industries competitive.

00:24:35.660 --> 00:24:38.935 align:middle line:84%
And they were looking for
these broader truths that

00:24:38.935 --> 00:24:41.060 align:middle line:84%
would give us-- instead of
having to study industry

00:24:41.060 --> 00:24:46.130 align:middle line:84%
by industry, what determines
profit levels in general?

00:24:46.130 --> 00:24:49.010 align:middle line:84%
Anyway, so regressions
here, there's

00:24:49.010 --> 00:24:51.950 align:middle line:84%
a data set the Census
of Manufacturers--

00:24:51.950 --> 00:24:54.745 align:middle line:84%
Census of Manufacturers, it's
a census of manufacturing firms

00:24:54.745 --> 00:24:55.620 align:middle line:90%
in the United States.

00:24:55.620 --> 00:24:58.895 align:middle line:84%
So it has data on every
individual firm in the United

00:24:58.895 --> 00:25:00.770 align:middle line:84%
States, and they send
them a big, long survey

00:25:00.770 --> 00:25:03.050 align:middle line:90%
with hundreds of questions.

00:25:03.050 --> 00:25:04.910 align:middle line:84%
If you ever want to
write papers using

00:25:04.910 --> 00:25:06.410 align:middle line:84%
the Census of
Manufacturers, you can

00:25:06.410 --> 00:25:08.840 align:middle line:84%
access the Census of
Manufacturers individual data

00:25:08.840 --> 00:25:13.100 align:middle line:84%
from the NBER, from a small,
windowless, unpleasant room

00:25:13.100 --> 00:25:13.920 align:middle line:90%
at the NBER.

00:25:13.920 --> 00:25:16.370 align:middle line:84%
But there is a
windowless room where

00:25:16.370 --> 00:25:19.010 align:middle line:84%
you can access the firm-level
data on every manufacturing

00:25:19.010 --> 00:25:20.870 align:middle line:90%
firm in the US.

00:25:20.870 --> 00:25:24.590 align:middle line:84%
People in that era didn't really
access the census microdata.

00:25:24.590 --> 00:25:27.590 align:middle line:84%
They just got the published
census data of means of these

00:25:27.590 --> 00:25:31.250 align:middle line:84%
variables for each of the
153-digit manufacturing

00:25:31.250 --> 00:25:34.513 align:middle line:84%
industries or 454-digit
manufacturing industries.

00:25:34.513 --> 00:25:36.180 align:middle line:84%
They'd run these
regressions, and they'd

00:25:36.180 --> 00:25:38.610 align:middle line:90%
talk about what determined--

00:25:38.610 --> 00:25:41.370 align:middle line:84%
how many firms did you need
for a markup to be competitive,

00:25:41.370 --> 00:25:43.740 align:middle line:84%
or how did competitiveness
vary with the number of firms

00:25:43.740 --> 00:25:44.407 align:middle line:90%
in the industry?

00:25:44.407 --> 00:25:49.400 align:middle line:90%


00:25:49.400 --> 00:25:53.090 align:middle line:84%
This was popular in the '60s
and '70s, and then it started

00:25:53.090 --> 00:25:54.080 align:middle line:90%
to become--

00:25:54.080 --> 00:25:57.110 align:middle line:84%
a number of critiques
of this approach

00:25:57.110 --> 00:25:59.510 align:middle line:90%
became very well accepted.

00:25:59.510 --> 00:26:03.920 align:middle line:84%
One critique was that
just all these studies

00:26:03.920 --> 00:26:07.203 align:middle line:84%
are leaving out very important
features of the industry.

00:26:07.203 --> 00:26:09.620 align:middle line:84%
So for instance, if I'm going
to study the steel industry,

00:26:09.620 --> 00:26:12.620 align:middle line:84%
I can't talk about prices
in the steel industry

00:26:12.620 --> 00:26:15.980 align:middle line:84%
or profits in the steel industry
without having price of iron ore

00:26:15.980 --> 00:26:17.280 align:middle line:90%
on the right side.

00:26:17.280 --> 00:26:19.280 align:middle line:84%
I can't think about prices
in the steel industry

00:26:19.280 --> 00:26:21.420 align:middle line:84%
without price of coke and
coal on the right side.

00:26:21.420 --> 00:26:22.837 align:middle line:84%
I can't think about
steel industry

00:26:22.837 --> 00:26:25.850 align:middle line:84%
without strength of union
labor in the Midwestern United

00:26:25.850 --> 00:26:27.210 align:middle line:90%
States on the right side.

00:26:27.210 --> 00:26:29.258 align:middle line:84%
So for every industry
I had, I would

00:26:29.258 --> 00:26:30.800 align:middle line:84%
think of three or
four or five things

00:26:30.800 --> 00:26:32.342 align:middle line:84%
that just had to be
on the right side

00:26:32.342 --> 00:26:33.740 align:middle line:90%
to talk about that industry.

00:26:33.740 --> 00:26:35.748 align:middle line:84%
But if you've got
150 industries,

00:26:35.748 --> 00:26:37.790 align:middle line:84%
and each industry brings
in three or four or five

00:26:37.790 --> 00:26:39.792 align:middle line:84%
unique things to put
on the right side,

00:26:39.792 --> 00:26:41.750 align:middle line:84%
and then you put them
all in on the right side,

00:26:41.750 --> 00:26:43.850 align:middle line:84%
you end up with a
regression with 750 things

00:26:43.850 --> 00:26:46.660 align:middle line:84%
on the right side
and 150 observations.

00:26:46.660 --> 00:26:49.722 align:middle line:84%
And obviously, now
machine learning,

00:26:49.722 --> 00:26:51.430 align:middle line:84%
people will tell you
that's totally fine.

00:26:51.430 --> 00:26:55.050 align:middle line:84%
750 explanatory variables, 150
observations, it all works fine.

00:26:55.050 --> 00:26:57.850 align:middle line:84%
But at least in the
1980s, that did not work.

00:26:57.850 --> 00:27:00.990 align:middle line:84%
You couldn't run
regressions like that.

00:27:00.990 --> 00:27:03.510 align:middle line:84%
The other problem that
came to be accepted

00:27:03.510 --> 00:27:09.480 align:middle line:84%
is that the census data, like
when you're studying markups,

00:27:09.480 --> 00:27:11.760 align:middle line:84%
markup in industry
i was really price

00:27:11.760 --> 00:27:18.150 align:middle line:84%
in industry i minus the cost
of production in industry i.

00:27:18.150 --> 00:27:22.830 align:middle line:84%
Or maybe it was price
minus cost over price.

00:27:22.830 --> 00:27:26.370 align:middle line:84%
But you would need to
use this data on what's

00:27:26.370 --> 00:27:28.200 align:middle line:90%
the cost of production.

00:27:28.200 --> 00:27:30.360 align:middle line:84%
And the cost of
production that you

00:27:30.360 --> 00:27:34.980 align:middle line:84%
get when managers put in for the
cost what their accountant told

00:27:34.980 --> 00:27:37.020 align:middle line:84%
them the cost is, is
very different from what

00:27:37.020 --> 00:27:40.320 align:middle line:84%
you would get if an economist
asked what the cost is.

00:27:40.320 --> 00:27:43.980 align:middle line:84%
For an economist, you really
want the marginal cost, not

00:27:43.980 --> 00:27:45.540 align:middle line:90%
the average cost.

00:27:45.540 --> 00:27:47.520 align:middle line:84%
But then even though
when you think about--

00:27:47.520 --> 00:27:50.910 align:middle line:84%
even getting the marginal
versus average, accounting costs

00:27:50.910 --> 00:27:53.190 align:middle line:84%
of all these
conventions in of how

00:27:53.190 --> 00:27:54.930 align:middle line:84%
do you treat the
cost of capital,

00:27:54.930 --> 00:27:56.790 align:middle line:84%
and do you amortize
it over time,

00:27:56.790 --> 00:27:59.640 align:middle line:84%
and you put in a
depreciation adjustment.

00:27:59.640 --> 00:28:01.890 align:middle line:84%
And what do you do when the
firm spends a lot of money

00:28:01.890 --> 00:28:02.860 align:middle line:90%
on advertising?

00:28:02.860 --> 00:28:04.500 align:middle line:84%
Is that a one-period
cost, or is that

00:28:04.500 --> 00:28:07.890 align:middle line:84%
a flow cost that depreciates
over the number of times

00:28:07.890 --> 00:28:10.060 align:middle line:90%
that the goodwill stock decays?

00:28:10.060 --> 00:28:12.390 align:middle line:90%
And so the costs--

00:28:12.390 --> 00:28:14.045 align:middle line:84%
a lot of the
elements of the cost,

00:28:14.045 --> 00:28:16.170 align:middle line:84%
how do you put the firm
overhead and all the people

00:28:16.170 --> 00:28:19.080 align:middle line:84%
who work in the management
office or whatever?

00:28:19.080 --> 00:28:21.600 align:middle line:84%
The costs have a
lot of assumptions

00:28:21.600 --> 00:28:23.520 align:middle line:90%
that go into defining them.

00:28:23.520 --> 00:28:27.510 align:middle line:84%
And so the difference
between the accounting cost

00:28:27.510 --> 00:28:30.210 align:middle line:90%
and the true cost is large.

00:28:30.210 --> 00:28:33.570 align:middle line:84%
And it's probably systematically
related to things like

00:28:33.570 --> 00:28:36.030 align:middle line:84%
whether the firm is doing a
lot of advertising or not,

00:28:36.030 --> 00:28:39.060 align:middle line:84%
whether it's doing a
lot of R&D, how old

00:28:39.060 --> 00:28:41.220 align:middle line:84%
the product is,
whether it's produced

00:28:41.220 --> 00:28:42.820 align:middle line:84%
in big factories or
little factories.

00:28:42.820 --> 00:28:45.180 align:middle line:84%
So all the stuff that you're
putting on the right side

00:28:45.180 --> 00:28:48.040 align:middle line:84%
is correlated with the
measurement error that you want.

00:28:48.040 --> 00:28:50.340 align:middle line:84%
And so then are we really
just finding correlations

00:28:50.340 --> 00:28:52.590 align:middle line:84%
with measurement error, or
are we finding correlations

00:28:52.590 --> 00:28:55.380 align:middle line:90%
with actual firm conduct?

00:28:55.380 --> 00:28:59.670 align:middle line:90%
And so that was a big issue.

00:28:59.670 --> 00:29:01.840 align:middle line:90%
Endogeneity is also a big issue.

00:29:01.840 --> 00:29:05.230 align:middle line:84%
You're putting the number
of firms on the right side.

00:29:05.230 --> 00:29:07.690 align:middle line:84%
Something you get taught over
and over again in classes

00:29:07.690 --> 00:29:10.570 align:middle line:84%
like this is, well, if
you have a business that's

00:29:10.570 --> 00:29:13.000 align:middle line:84%
highly profitable, then more
firms are going to enter,

00:29:13.000 --> 00:29:15.010 align:middle line:84%
and that's going to
change the profits.

00:29:15.010 --> 00:29:18.415 align:middle line:84%
So this four-firm
concentration ratio

00:29:18.415 --> 00:29:20.290 align:middle line:84%
is also going to be
endogenous, or the number

00:29:20.290 --> 00:29:22.810 align:middle line:84%
of firms on the right side
is going to be endogenous.

00:29:22.810 --> 00:29:24.970 align:middle line:84%
There really wasn't
much in the way of IV

00:29:24.970 --> 00:29:28.900 align:middle line:84%
estimation in the structure
conduct performance literature.

00:29:28.900 --> 00:29:34.540 align:middle line:84%
And then there was also
interest in IO theory.

00:29:34.540 --> 00:29:39.710 align:middle line:84%
This led to what was
called the NEIO revolution.

00:29:39.710 --> 00:29:42.800 align:middle line:84%
So NEIO stands for New Empirical
Industrial Organization.

00:29:42.800 --> 00:29:44.950 align:middle line:84%
I guess lots of fields
run into this problem.

00:29:44.950 --> 00:29:48.077 align:middle line:84%
You have the sort
of new something,

00:29:48.077 --> 00:29:49.910 align:middle line:84%
and then there's something
comes after that.

00:29:49.910 --> 00:29:51.110 align:middle line:84%
Like you have the
new trade theory,

00:29:51.110 --> 00:29:52.943 align:middle line:84%
and then you have to
have the new, new trade

00:29:52.943 --> 00:29:54.260 align:middle line:90%
theory or whatever, because--

00:29:54.260 --> 00:29:57.920 align:middle line:84%
anyway, so in the 19 early '80s,
people started to call this

00:29:57.920 --> 00:30:00.000 align:middle line:84%
the new empirical
industrial organization.

00:30:00.000 --> 00:30:01.040 align:middle line:90%
It's no longer very new.

00:30:01.040 --> 00:30:04.550 align:middle line:90%
But what happened in NEIO--

00:30:04.550 --> 00:30:07.400 align:middle line:84%
so one is, it went
back to the '60s.

00:30:07.400 --> 00:30:10.760 align:middle line:84%
It went back to people writing
papers about single industries.

00:30:10.760 --> 00:30:13.460 align:middle line:84%
Everyone thinks that there
are a lot of specific things

00:30:13.460 --> 00:30:14.940 align:middle line:84%
about every industry
that matters,

00:30:14.940 --> 00:30:17.000 align:middle line:84%
so we need to study it
one industry at a time

00:30:17.000 --> 00:30:18.740 align:middle line:84%
and control for all
those factors that

00:30:18.740 --> 00:30:21.620 align:middle line:90%
should be on the right side.

00:30:21.620 --> 00:30:25.080 align:middle line:84%
There was an emphasis on
using what was well measured.

00:30:25.080 --> 00:30:26.570 align:middle line:90%
So it's sort of funny.

00:30:26.570 --> 00:30:29.477 align:middle line:84%
In macro, they think
cost is well measured,

00:30:29.477 --> 00:30:31.310 align:middle line:84%
and price is hard to
measure, so they always

00:30:31.310 --> 00:30:32.660 align:middle line:90%
want to use cost data.

00:30:32.660 --> 00:30:35.480 align:middle line:84%
In IO, people think that
price is well measured,

00:30:35.480 --> 00:30:38.900 align:middle line:84%
cost is hard to measure, so
we always use price data.

00:30:38.900 --> 00:30:43.100 align:middle line:84%
So there's this emphasis on
using quantities, prices,

00:30:43.100 --> 00:30:45.920 align:middle line:84%
number of firms, market
shares, those things

00:30:45.920 --> 00:30:49.200 align:middle line:84%
as the explanatory variables--
as the variables you use

00:30:49.200 --> 00:30:52.950 align:middle line:84%
rather than
accounting variables.

00:30:52.950 --> 00:30:55.410 align:middle line:84%
Great deal of attention
paid now to identification

00:30:55.410 --> 00:30:56.760 align:middle line:90%
and endogeneity.

00:30:56.760 --> 00:31:01.570 align:middle line:84%
And theory also came to take
on a big role in empirical IO.

00:31:01.570 --> 00:31:04.300 align:middle line:84%
And it's often that the--
two different reasons.

00:31:04.300 --> 00:31:08.820 align:middle line:84%
One is that the empirical work
is often designed to improve

00:31:08.820 --> 00:31:11.190 align:middle line:84%
our understanding of
some theoretical model,

00:31:11.190 --> 00:31:13.720 align:middle line:84%
which were being recently
developed in the '80s.

00:31:13.720 --> 00:31:16.568 align:middle line:84%
And then the second is
because even if you're not

00:31:16.568 --> 00:31:18.360 align:middle line:84%
trying to comment on
the theory, the theory

00:31:18.360 --> 00:31:21.722 align:middle line:84%
is going to give you
thoughts about what

00:31:21.722 --> 00:31:23.430 align:middle line:84%
the functional form
should be or what you

00:31:23.430 --> 00:31:25.050 align:middle line:90%
should be regressing on what.

00:31:25.050 --> 00:31:27.180 align:middle line:84%
And the papers
are often designed

00:31:27.180 --> 00:31:29.218 align:middle line:84%
to test some particular--
or enlightened

00:31:29.218 --> 00:31:31.260 align:middle line:84%
by some particular theory
when they're setting up

00:31:31.260 --> 00:31:32.630 align:middle line:90%
what analyses they're doing.

00:31:32.630 --> 00:31:36.060 align:middle line:90%


00:31:36.060 --> 00:31:37.920 align:middle line:84%
More recently-- so
at the beginning

00:31:37.920 --> 00:31:40.890 align:middle line:84%
of the NEIO revolution,
people would sometimes

00:31:40.890 --> 00:31:42.133 align:middle line:90%
describe work as structural.

00:31:42.133 --> 00:31:43.800 align:middle line:84%
And they would use
the word "structural"

00:31:43.800 --> 00:31:47.160 align:middle line:84%
in a way that's different from
what structural means today.

00:31:47.160 --> 00:31:50.910 align:middle line:84%
So in the '80s, structural
really meant instead

00:31:50.910 --> 00:31:54.540 align:middle line:84%
of estimating equilibrium
objects like understanding price

00:31:54.540 --> 00:31:57.600 align:middle line:84%
as a function of things,
you're understanding supply

00:31:57.600 --> 00:32:00.090 align:middle line:84%
as a function of things and
demand as a function of things.

00:32:00.090 --> 00:32:01.950 align:middle line:84%
And the underlying
equations that

00:32:01.950 --> 00:32:03.840 align:middle line:84%
determine the
equilibrium instead

00:32:03.840 --> 00:32:06.030 align:middle line:90%
of analyzing the equilibrium.

00:32:06.030 --> 00:32:08.370 align:middle line:84%
I think, over time,
structural work

00:32:08.370 --> 00:32:12.090 align:middle line:84%
has increasingly come to be
used to describe papers that

00:32:12.090 --> 00:32:15.390 align:middle line:84%
assume that some
theoretical model is correct

00:32:15.390 --> 00:32:18.300 align:middle line:84%
and then focus on
estimating the parameters

00:32:18.300 --> 00:32:20.080 align:middle line:90%
of that theoretical model.

00:32:20.080 --> 00:32:22.680 align:middle line:84%
And the word "structural" is
also increasingly used to talk

00:32:22.680 --> 00:32:26.110 align:middle line:84%
about work that goes all the way
to estimating the primitives.

00:32:26.110 --> 00:32:28.740 align:middle line:84%
In some ways, every
industry, the outcomes

00:32:28.740 --> 00:32:33.720 align:middle line:84%
depend on the production
functions of the firms,

00:32:33.720 --> 00:32:36.360 align:middle line:84%
the utilities of the
consumers, and then something

00:32:36.360 --> 00:32:39.312 align:middle line:84%
about the strategies that
are available to the firms.

00:32:39.312 --> 00:32:40.770 align:middle line:84%
And so structural
work increasingly

00:32:40.770 --> 00:32:43.920 align:middle line:84%
is trying to go back
to those primitives.

00:32:43.920 --> 00:32:45.955 align:middle line:84%
Instead of just estimating
supply and demand,

00:32:45.955 --> 00:32:47.580 align:middle line:84%
you're trying to
estimate distributions

00:32:47.580 --> 00:32:50.250 align:middle line:90%
of consumer utility functions.

00:32:50.250 --> 00:32:54.710 align:middle line:84%
Something that's very popular
in IO papers is that you--

00:32:54.710 --> 00:32:57.420 align:middle line:84%
a reason for getting all
the way to the primitives

00:32:57.420 --> 00:33:00.510 align:middle line:84%
is then you have these
counterfactual simulations where

00:33:00.510 --> 00:33:03.790 align:middle line:84%
you say, I observe this
industry in this state.

00:33:03.790 --> 00:33:06.190 align:middle line:84%
What would happen if there
was entry in that industry?

00:33:06.190 --> 00:33:08.530 align:middle line:84%
What would happen if we
broke up these firms?

00:33:08.530 --> 00:33:11.760 align:middle line:84%
What would happen if we put
in a price cap regulation?

00:33:11.760 --> 00:33:13.710 align:middle line:84%
Once you know these
primitives, you

00:33:13.710 --> 00:33:16.560 align:middle line:84%
can then talk about here are
all these alternate policies we

00:33:16.560 --> 00:33:19.840 align:middle line:84%
might implement, and
then what would that do.

00:33:19.840 --> 00:33:21.480 align:middle line:84%
And in some sense,
this is what's

00:33:21.480 --> 00:33:25.110 align:middle line:84%
really become very popular
in many fields like education

00:33:25.110 --> 00:33:28.340 align:middle line:84%
and development, where you
estimate a fundamental model,

00:33:28.340 --> 00:33:30.930 align:middle line:84%
and then you talk about I
did a trial of this one,

00:33:30.930 --> 00:33:33.130 align:middle line:84%
I did a randomized
trial of this policy.

00:33:33.130 --> 00:33:34.740 align:middle line:84%
I can also do an
empirical estimate

00:33:34.740 --> 00:33:37.530 align:middle line:84%
of what other policies
would have done because I've

00:33:37.530 --> 00:33:40.656 align:middle line:90%
uncovered the primitives.

00:33:40.656 --> 00:33:43.170 align:middle line:84%
And I would say what's also
happening at the same time--

00:33:43.170 --> 00:33:46.110 align:middle line:84%
while there's a lot of good
structural work, over time,

00:33:46.110 --> 00:33:49.260 align:middle line:84%
structural-empirical IO has
become less and less connected

00:33:49.260 --> 00:33:50.020 align:middle line:90%
to theory.

00:33:50.020 --> 00:33:52.440 align:middle line:84%
And in some sense,
structural-empirical IO models

00:33:52.440 --> 00:33:55.080 align:middle line:84%
these days tend
to use structures

00:33:55.080 --> 00:33:57.090 align:middle line:84%
that previous
structural-empirical papers have

00:33:57.090 --> 00:34:00.570 align:middle line:84%
used rather than the structures
of the theoretical models

00:34:00.570 --> 00:34:03.310 align:middle line:84%
that theorists would use to
describe the same situation.

00:34:03.310 --> 00:34:06.780 align:middle line:84%
And part of that is because
repeated games really

00:34:06.780 --> 00:34:08.813 align:middle line:84%
don't work very well
for empirical work.

00:34:08.813 --> 00:34:10.980 align:middle line:84%
Incomplete information
doesn't really work very well

00:34:10.980 --> 00:34:11.860 align:middle line:90%
for empirical work.

00:34:11.860 --> 00:34:14.159 align:middle line:84%
And so empirical work tends
to have these models that

00:34:14.159 --> 00:34:18.060 align:middle line:84%
have this very limited
sense of dynamic competition

00:34:18.060 --> 00:34:20.370 align:middle line:84%
and of incomplete
information and other factors

00:34:20.370 --> 00:34:25.440 align:middle line:84%
because it's not what you can
estimate well structurally--

00:34:25.440 --> 00:34:27.190 align:middle line:84%
although, obviously,
there's a lot of work

00:34:27.190 --> 00:34:29.230 align:middle line:90%
trying to do that better.

00:34:29.230 --> 00:34:33.940 align:middle line:84%
So as far as how do you come up
with a topic for an empirical IO

00:34:33.940 --> 00:34:37.965 align:middle line:84%
paper, people do multiple
different things.

00:34:37.965 --> 00:34:39.340 align:middle line:84%
One thing that
you can always do,

00:34:39.340 --> 00:34:41.170 align:middle line:84%
just like in any
other applied field,

00:34:41.170 --> 00:34:44.050 align:middle line:84%
is just come up with some
applied or policy question

00:34:44.050 --> 00:34:49.239 align:middle line:84%
and say, what can I
teach people about that.

00:34:49.239 --> 00:34:51.880 align:middle line:84%
A second thing one can do
is improve understanding

00:34:51.880 --> 00:34:53.270 align:middle line:90%
of some theoretical model.

00:34:53.270 --> 00:34:58.010 align:middle line:84%
There's recent work on this
monopoly information design.

00:34:58.010 --> 00:35:00.470 align:middle line:84%
Can I do some empirical
work to understand,

00:35:00.470 --> 00:35:02.560 align:middle line:84%
does information design
work in practice,

00:35:02.560 --> 00:35:05.050 align:middle line:84%
or how well does it work
or does it not work?

00:35:05.050 --> 00:35:06.910 align:middle line:84%
The other third thing
you can do is also do

00:35:06.910 --> 00:35:09.140 align:middle line:84%
an improvement on existing
empirical technique.

00:35:09.140 --> 00:35:11.140 align:middle line:84%
If there are many
previous papers trying

00:35:11.140 --> 00:35:14.540 align:middle line:84%
to estimate empirical
models of auctions,

00:35:14.540 --> 00:35:16.810 align:middle line:84%
I have some better
way to estimate

00:35:16.810 --> 00:35:18.102 align:middle line:90%
empirical model of an auction.

00:35:18.102 --> 00:35:19.810 align:middle line:84%
And you try to find
a data set to improve

00:35:19.810 --> 00:35:21.186 align:middle line:90%
on that empirical technique.

00:35:21.186 --> 00:35:24.340 align:middle line:90%


00:35:24.340 --> 00:35:26.298 align:middle line:90%
How do you find your topic?

00:35:26.298 --> 00:35:28.090 align:middle line:84%
Obviously, if you have
an applied question,

00:35:28.090 --> 00:35:30.940 align:middle line:84%
you look for the
data to find it.

00:35:30.940 --> 00:35:34.090 align:middle line:84%
If you're trying to do something
improving understanding

00:35:34.090 --> 00:35:36.490 align:middle line:84%
of a theoretical
model, this is where

00:35:36.490 --> 00:35:38.480 align:middle line:84%
we get papers on
ketchup and cardboard.

00:35:38.480 --> 00:35:41.680 align:middle line:84%
It's like, it's very
complicated to estimate anything

00:35:41.680 --> 00:35:44.635 align:middle line:84%
in the mobile phone environment
or the computer environment

00:35:44.635 --> 00:35:46.510 align:middle line:84%
because there's so many
different firms doing

00:35:46.510 --> 00:35:48.532 align:middle line:90%
so many different things.

00:35:48.532 --> 00:35:50.740 align:middle line:84%
If you're trying to study
some topic and you're like,

00:35:50.740 --> 00:35:53.330 align:middle line:84%
I could study this in the
cardboard box industry,

00:35:53.330 --> 00:35:56.080 align:middle line:84%
the cardboard box industry is
an incredibly simple industry.

00:35:56.080 --> 00:36:01.210 align:middle line:84%
Basically, you take paper
or you take leftover waste

00:36:01.210 --> 00:36:07.060 align:middle line:84%
from wood mills, you mush
it together, you add water,

00:36:07.060 --> 00:36:09.400 align:middle line:90%
you dry it.

00:36:09.400 --> 00:36:11.750 align:middle line:84%
There's just, like,
three ingredients.

00:36:11.750 --> 00:36:13.360 align:middle line:90%
There's nothing much there.

00:36:13.360 --> 00:36:14.748 align:middle line:90%
You have shipping costs.

00:36:14.748 --> 00:36:17.290 align:middle line:84%
You can estimate what it costs
to ship cardboard from point A

00:36:17.290 --> 00:36:18.380 align:middle line:90%
to point B.

00:36:18.380 --> 00:36:20.800 align:middle line:84%
If you find an industry
that strips away

00:36:20.800 --> 00:36:23.270 align:middle line:84%
all the complicated
stuff going on,

00:36:23.270 --> 00:36:25.190 align:middle line:84%
there aren't agreements
with Google doing

00:36:25.190 --> 00:36:26.430 align:middle line:90%
this for some other reason.

00:36:26.430 --> 00:36:27.870 align:middle line:90%
It's just cardboard.

00:36:27.870 --> 00:36:31.370 align:middle line:84%
So sometimes you can
pick a simple industry

00:36:31.370 --> 00:36:34.130 align:middle line:84%
in order to study a
complicated model because you

00:36:34.130 --> 00:36:36.890 align:middle line:84%
want to isolate that
theoretical factor that you're

00:36:36.890 --> 00:36:38.720 align:middle line:90%
interested in.

00:36:38.720 --> 00:36:44.480 align:middle line:84%
Third thing you can
always do is find data.

00:36:44.480 --> 00:36:46.230 align:middle line:90%
Data is always in short supply.

00:36:46.230 --> 00:36:48.570 align:middle line:84%
IO papers tend to
collect their own data.

00:36:48.570 --> 00:36:50.930 align:middle line:84%
So you have a friend
who works at a startup

00:36:50.930 --> 00:36:53.300 align:middle line:84%
and is willing to give you
individual-level consumer

00:36:53.300 --> 00:36:55.690 align:middle line:90%
data on something or other.

00:36:55.690 --> 00:36:58.190 align:middle line:84%
I tend to think, you look at
all the topics in this course--

00:36:58.190 --> 00:36:59.090 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to talk
about monopoly,

00:36:59.090 --> 00:37:00.480 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to talk about
price discrimination,

00:37:00.480 --> 00:37:01.340 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to talk
about entry, and I'm

00:37:01.340 --> 00:37:02.715 align:middle line:84%
going to talk
about search costs.

00:37:02.715 --> 00:37:05.600 align:middle line:84%
And there must be some of
those things that are involved

00:37:05.600 --> 00:37:06.810 align:middle line:90%
in your friend's business.

00:37:06.810 --> 00:37:09.260 align:middle line:84%
So if you can get the
really, really rich microdata

00:37:09.260 --> 00:37:12.547 align:middle line:84%
that no one has, there must
be some interesting comment

00:37:12.547 --> 00:37:14.130 align:middle line:84%
about that industry
that you can make.

00:37:14.130 --> 00:37:15.950 align:middle line:84%
There must be something
that that data

00:37:15.950 --> 00:37:17.340 align:middle line:90%
set is good for studying.

00:37:17.340 --> 00:37:20.810 align:middle line:84%
And so that's another way
to think about paper topics.

00:37:20.810 --> 00:37:25.520 align:middle line:84%
So I would say that
somehow, back to the 1970s,

00:37:25.520 --> 00:37:29.000 align:middle line:84%
there has also been this recent
revival of interest in IO in big

00:37:29.000 --> 00:37:33.200 align:middle line:84%
questions, in part driven by
macroeconomists asking big IO

00:37:33.200 --> 00:37:36.080 align:middle line:84%
questions because the IO
people haven't been doing it.

00:37:36.080 --> 00:37:38.480 align:middle line:84%
And so there has been a big
question in these questions

00:37:38.480 --> 00:37:44.360 align:middle line:84%
like, Has competition declined
in the US economy as a whole?

00:37:44.360 --> 00:37:46.220 align:middle line:84%
And do we believe
that there's been

00:37:46.220 --> 00:37:48.830 align:middle line:84%
this increase in
mark-ups or drop

00:37:48.830 --> 00:37:52.940 align:middle line:84%
in concentration over
the last decades?

00:37:52.940 --> 00:37:56.000 align:middle line:84%
So two papers are both--
these are both going

00:37:56.000 --> 00:37:57.860 align:middle line:90%
to be classic NEIO papers.

00:37:57.860 --> 00:38:00.440 align:middle line:84%
So they're going to have
industry-specific questions

00:38:00.440 --> 00:38:01.260 align:middle line:90%
of interest.

00:38:01.260 --> 00:38:03.890 align:middle line:84%
And they're also going to
have theoretical models

00:38:03.890 --> 00:38:08.840 align:middle line:84%
that they're either trying to
comment on or use as guidance.

00:38:08.840 --> 00:38:11.840 align:middle line:84%
The first one is, I
would say, really--

00:38:11.840 --> 00:38:15.080 align:middle line:84%
it's an industry-specific
question of interest.

00:38:15.080 --> 00:38:18.080 align:middle line:84%
It's also, more than
that, a paper that's

00:38:18.080 --> 00:38:20.240 align:middle line:84%
about understanding
theoretical models

00:38:20.240 --> 00:38:21.900 align:middle line:84%
and whether they
really apply or not.

00:38:21.900 --> 00:38:24.260 align:middle line:84%
So the paper's, "Are
durable goods consumers

00:38:24.260 --> 00:38:27.320 align:middle line:84%
forward-looking-- evidence
from college textbooks."

00:38:27.320 --> 00:38:29.390 align:middle line:84%
To the extent there are
policy questions here,

00:38:29.390 --> 00:38:30.860 align:middle line:84%
these should be
familiar with you.

00:38:30.860 --> 00:38:36.740 align:middle line:84%
One policy question is, Why is
it that textbooks cost $300?

00:38:36.740 --> 00:38:39.210 align:middle line:84%
We know that you can
print a book for 20 bucks.

00:38:39.210 --> 00:38:40.570 align:middle line:90%
Why do they cost 300?

00:38:40.570 --> 00:38:42.040 align:middle line:84%
Is there something
wrong with that?

00:38:42.040 --> 00:38:43.470 align:middle line:84%
And the other thing I don't
know if you experienced

00:38:43.470 --> 00:38:46.440 align:middle line:84%
as an undergraduate is, Why is
it that this book that was only

00:38:46.440 --> 00:38:49.980 align:middle line:84%
2-and-1/2 years old is now
already having a new edition

00:38:49.980 --> 00:38:53.100 align:middle line:84%
come out, and I can't sell my
old book back to the bookstore,

00:38:53.100 --> 00:38:55.860 align:middle line:84%
which I had bought for
$300, and it's now worthless

00:38:55.860 --> 00:38:59.370 align:middle line:84%
because the 14th edition of
somebody's book just came out

00:38:59.370 --> 00:39:01.900 align:middle line:84%
replacing the 13th
edition three years later?

00:39:01.900 --> 00:39:04.530 align:middle line:84%
Is this some kind
of scam or whatever

00:39:04.530 --> 00:39:06.340 align:middle line:84%
the firms are doing
that there's something

00:39:06.340 --> 00:39:08.010 align:middle line:84%
about the durable goods
sales where they bring out

00:39:08.010 --> 00:39:10.140 align:middle line:84%
these new books over
and over and over again

00:39:10.140 --> 00:39:11.910 align:middle line:84%
to earn greater
profits than they could

00:39:11.910 --> 00:39:14.130 align:middle line:84%
if they just brought out
books at an efficient time

00:39:14.130 --> 00:39:15.620 align:middle line:90%
scale, whatever that means?

00:39:15.620 --> 00:39:22.325 align:middle line:90%


00:39:22.325 --> 00:39:24.520 align:middle line:84%
So I think I said
most of these things.

00:39:24.520 --> 00:39:27.430 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, so obviously, the
theory-related question

00:39:27.430 --> 00:39:32.460 align:middle line:84%
is, Are these durable goods
models relevant in practice?

00:39:32.460 --> 00:39:35.530 align:middle line:84%
In particular, are consumers
forward-looking and

00:39:35.530 --> 00:39:40.158 align:middle line:84%
well-designed and solving
dynamic optimization problems

00:39:40.158 --> 00:39:41.950 align:middle line:84%
when they're doing
things, which always you

00:39:41.950 --> 00:39:45.130 align:middle line:84%
think about students are so
bad at answering questions

00:39:45.130 --> 00:39:47.690 align:middle line:84%
on my exams about dynamic
durable good pricing.

00:39:47.690 --> 00:39:50.290 align:middle line:84%
But somehow, we always
assume that they always

00:39:50.290 --> 00:39:52.480 align:middle line:84%
know how to do that for
themselves in real life

00:39:52.480 --> 00:39:54.910 align:middle line:84%
and think about the
future discounted value

00:39:54.910 --> 00:39:58.300 align:middle line:84%
when they're making any
purchases that they're making.

00:39:58.300 --> 00:40:00.276 align:middle line:90%
Is that a good assumption?

00:40:00.276 --> 00:40:02.890 align:middle line:90%


00:40:02.890 --> 00:40:04.780 align:middle line:84%
The choice to study
this, if you're

00:40:04.780 --> 00:40:12.160 align:middle line:84%
trying to study durable goods
and forward-looking consumers,

00:40:12.160 --> 00:40:14.740 align:middle line:84%
this paper, you can see
why they do what they do.

00:40:14.740 --> 00:40:17.860 align:middle line:84%
First is, in addition to
studying durable goods,

00:40:17.860 --> 00:40:20.570 align:middle line:84%
they do get an industry-specific
question of interest

00:40:20.570 --> 00:40:21.280 align:middle line:90%
at the same time.

00:40:21.280 --> 00:40:25.060 align:middle line:84%
It's good to do two things
instead of one in a paper.

00:40:25.060 --> 00:40:28.420 align:middle line:84%
There are aspects of
this textbook example

00:40:28.420 --> 00:40:30.010 align:middle line:84%
where the simplicity
of the business

00:40:30.010 --> 00:40:31.280 align:middle line:90%
makes the modeling easier.

00:40:31.280 --> 00:40:34.945 align:middle line:84%
So, for instance, all consumers
have to either buy the textbook

00:40:34.945 --> 00:40:36.820 align:middle line:84%
in the first week of
the semester or the week

00:40:36.820 --> 00:40:39.590 align:middle line:84%
before the semester
starts or not at all.

00:40:39.590 --> 00:40:42.760 align:middle line:84%
There's none of this "I can wait
till 7 weeks into the semester

00:40:42.760 --> 00:40:46.060 align:middle line:84%
and buy the book or buy it a
semester after I take the course

00:40:46.060 --> 00:40:48.920 align:middle line:84%
to take advantage of the price
declines or things like that."

00:40:48.920 --> 00:40:51.670 align:middle line:84%
It just-- it's a
static decision.

00:40:51.670 --> 00:40:53.770 align:middle line:84%
The other thing is that
they only buy the textbook

00:40:53.770 --> 00:40:54.850 align:middle line:90%
that you're assigned.

00:40:54.850 --> 00:40:57.580 align:middle line:84%
If you're assigned by
Pindyck and Rubinfeld's

00:40:57.580 --> 00:41:00.250 align:middle line:84%
intermediate micro textbook,
you don't think about,

00:41:00.250 --> 00:41:04.630 align:middle line:84%
OK, instead of buying
Pindyck and Rubinfeld,

00:41:04.630 --> 00:41:06.910 align:middle line:84%
I could buy Bernheim
and Winston,

00:41:06.910 --> 00:41:09.715 align:middle line:84%
or I could buy
whoever and Snyder.

00:41:09.715 --> 00:41:12.900 align:middle line:90%


00:41:12.900 --> 00:41:15.360 align:middle line:84%
In some sense,
students only make

00:41:15.360 --> 00:41:17.920 align:middle line:84%
the decision between two
products or three products--

00:41:17.920 --> 00:41:19.930 align:middle line:84%
buy the new copy of
Pindyck and Rubinfeld,

00:41:19.930 --> 00:41:22.420 align:middle line:84%
buy the used copy of Pindyck
and Rubinfeld, buy nothing.

00:41:22.420 --> 00:41:24.450 align:middle line:84%
And so you get this much
simpler demand system

00:41:24.450 --> 00:41:26.400 align:middle line:84%
than you would get
if students were

00:41:26.400 --> 00:41:32.030 align:middle line:84%
choosing from this menu of 57
different things they could buy.

00:41:32.030 --> 00:41:34.160 align:middle line:84%
We also can think about
things we can ignore,

00:41:34.160 --> 00:41:35.780 align:middle line:84%
like in many used
good markets, you'd

00:41:35.780 --> 00:41:37.880 align:middle line:90%
want to study a lemons problem.

00:41:37.880 --> 00:41:39.830 align:middle line:84%
What do consumers
think about the quality

00:41:39.830 --> 00:41:41.600 align:middle line:84%
of the used car
versus the new car,

00:41:41.600 --> 00:41:43.642 align:middle line:84%
and how is that impacting
them, and how does that

00:41:43.642 --> 00:41:44.610 align:middle line:90%
change over time?

00:41:44.610 --> 00:41:46.490 align:middle line:84%
Used textbooks,
especially if you buy them

00:41:46.490 --> 00:41:48.073 align:middle line:84%
in the college
bookstore, you can just

00:41:48.073 --> 00:41:51.440 align:middle line:84%
open them up, look at them, see
how much highlighting there is

00:41:51.440 --> 00:41:53.370 align:middle line:90%
in it, and then buy it or not.

00:41:53.370 --> 00:41:56.060 align:middle line:84%
There isn't this
asymmetric information

00:41:56.060 --> 00:41:57.535 align:middle line:90%
we would want to model.

00:41:57.535 --> 00:41:59.660 align:middle line:84%
And then the other thing
you're always trying to do

00:41:59.660 --> 00:42:07.140 align:middle line:84%
is get enough observations
to estimate your model.

00:42:07.140 --> 00:42:09.980 align:middle line:84%
And here, what's really nice
is that there's a very, very

00:42:09.980 --> 00:42:11.790 align:middle line:90%
large number of textbooks.

00:42:11.790 --> 00:42:16.250 align:middle line:84%
And so they have
three subjects--

00:42:16.250 --> 00:42:16.820 align:middle line:90%
what is it?

00:42:16.820 --> 00:42:19.770 align:middle line:84%
It's economics,
psychology, and biology.

00:42:19.770 --> 00:42:22.232 align:middle line:84%
They have 10 semesters in
which they can study it,

00:42:22.232 --> 00:42:24.440 align:middle line:84%
and then they have hundreds
and hundreds and hundreds

00:42:24.440 --> 00:42:25.980 align:middle line:90%
and hundreds of textbooks.

00:42:25.980 --> 00:42:29.870 align:middle line:84%
And so that combination of
semesters times subjects times

00:42:29.870 --> 00:42:33.490 align:middle line:84%
the textbooks is going to give
you many, many observations.

00:42:33.490 --> 00:42:35.280 align:middle line:84%
And you can-- there's
always this thing

00:42:35.280 --> 00:42:37.337 align:middle line:84%
of if you study 100
different products,

00:42:37.337 --> 00:42:39.420 align:middle line:84%
are those 100 different
products really identical?

00:42:39.420 --> 00:42:41.470 align:middle line:84%
Can you use the same
parameters for them?

00:42:41.470 --> 00:42:43.920 align:middle line:84%
You can think textbooks are
similar enough to each other

00:42:43.920 --> 00:42:46.860 align:middle line:84%
that the elasticity of demand
for Pindyck and Rubinfeld

00:42:46.860 --> 00:42:49.320 align:middle line:84%
and the elasticity of demand
for Bernheim and Winston

00:42:49.320 --> 00:42:50.903 align:middle line:84%
are probably the
same, and we can just

00:42:50.903 --> 00:42:52.800 align:middle line:84%
put one parameter in
there instead of having

00:42:52.800 --> 00:42:53.906 align:middle line:90%
hundreds of parameters.

00:42:53.906 --> 00:42:58.230 align:middle line:90%


00:42:58.230 --> 00:43:01.512 align:middle line:84%
Some background facts
before you start modeling--

00:43:01.512 --> 00:43:03.720 align:middle line:84%
it's always good to think
about things that are true.

00:43:03.720 --> 00:43:06.000 align:middle line:84%
Well, one is that
new editions come out

00:43:06.000 --> 00:43:08.850 align:middle line:90%
every three to five years.

00:43:08.850 --> 00:43:12.820 align:middle line:84%
Second fact is that textbook
prices seem to be fixed.

00:43:12.820 --> 00:43:15.615 align:middle line:84%
Why this is true is
kind of a puzzle.

00:43:15.615 --> 00:43:17.490 align:middle line:84%
Maybe you have to have
some reputation model,

00:43:17.490 --> 00:43:19.800 align:middle line:84%
but you might have
thought, OK, here's

00:43:19.800 --> 00:43:21.510 align:middle line:90%
how I'm going to do this test.

00:43:21.510 --> 00:43:24.463 align:middle line:84%
The book is going to
come out the first--

00:43:24.463 --> 00:43:25.880 align:middle line:84%
if I buy the book
when it's new, I

00:43:25.880 --> 00:43:28.620 align:middle line:84%
know I could rent it to
five future students.

00:43:28.620 --> 00:43:30.225 align:middle line:84%
If I buy the book
one semester, well,

00:43:30.225 --> 00:43:32.100 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to rent it to
four future students.

00:43:32.100 --> 00:43:33.642 align:middle line:84%
If I buy it, like,
four semesters in,

00:43:33.642 --> 00:43:35.550 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to rent
it once to somebody.

00:43:35.550 --> 00:43:38.250 align:middle line:84%
So there ought to be this real
decline in the prices of a book.

00:43:38.250 --> 00:43:40.520 align:middle line:84%
And a book that's
five semesters old

00:43:40.520 --> 00:43:42.590 align:middle line:84%
ought to be worth
one-fifth of a book that's

00:43:42.590 --> 00:43:45.800 align:middle line:84%
brand new because you can't keep
renting it over and over again.

00:43:45.800 --> 00:43:47.720 align:middle line:90%
Well, that just doesn't happen.

00:43:47.720 --> 00:43:50.160 align:middle line:90%
Textbook prices do not drop.

00:43:50.160 --> 00:43:52.860 align:middle line:90%
The book comes out, it's $350.

00:43:52.860 --> 00:43:55.590 align:middle line:90%
Five semesters later, it's $350.

00:43:55.590 --> 00:43:58.340 align:middle line:84%
So for whatever
reason, we're not

00:43:58.340 --> 00:44:01.310 align:middle line:84%
going to think of
optimal pricing.

00:44:01.310 --> 00:44:04.520 align:middle line:84%
Maybe it's a pricing puzzle,
but textbook prices just

00:44:04.520 --> 00:44:06.475 align:middle line:84%
don't change in
between editions.

00:44:06.475 --> 00:44:11.440 align:middle line:90%


00:44:11.440 --> 00:44:13.330 align:middle line:84%
I don't know to what
extent these are still

00:44:13.330 --> 00:44:14.380 align:middle line:90%
true in the internet era.

00:44:14.380 --> 00:44:17.500 align:middle line:84%
But at the time, the
used-to-new ratio

00:44:17.500 --> 00:44:22.990 align:middle line:90%
was standardized at 50% to 75%.

00:44:22.990 --> 00:44:24.190 align:middle line:90%
Does the Coop even sell--

00:44:24.190 --> 00:44:26.440 align:middle line:84%
you can still go to the Coop
and buy all the textbooks

00:44:26.440 --> 00:44:27.107 align:middle line:90%
for the courses?

00:44:27.107 --> 00:44:28.060 align:middle line:90%
Is that right?

00:44:28.060 --> 00:44:30.765 align:middle line:90%
So maybe no one does that, OK.

00:44:30.765 --> 00:44:33.070 align:middle line:84%
So anyway, if you wanted
to buy your books,

00:44:33.070 --> 00:44:34.390 align:middle line:90%
you would go to the MIT Coop.

00:44:34.390 --> 00:44:35.932 align:middle line:84%
Down in the basement
of the MIT Coop,

00:44:35.932 --> 00:44:37.510 align:middle line:90%
there'd be a bunch of textbooks.

00:44:37.510 --> 00:44:40.550 align:middle line:84%
MIT would have an agreement
with the bookstore.

00:44:40.550 --> 00:44:42.610 align:middle line:84%
In this case, we used
to own the bookstore,

00:44:42.610 --> 00:44:44.810 align:middle line:84%
but it's now Barnes
and Noble who owns it.

00:44:44.810 --> 00:44:46.930 align:middle line:84%
But they would sell
the used textbook

00:44:46.930 --> 00:44:50.680 align:middle line:84%
at, at most, 75% of the
price of the new textbook.

00:44:50.680 --> 00:44:52.210 align:middle line:84%
And then you would
also often have

00:44:52.210 --> 00:44:53.710 align:middle line:84%
an agreement with the
company that was selling

00:44:53.710 --> 00:44:54.710 align:middle line:90%
books in your bookstore.

00:44:54.710 --> 00:44:58.030 align:middle line:84%
If you want to buy back
used books at the MIT Coop,

00:44:58.030 --> 00:45:01.810 align:middle line:84%
you must pay 50% of the cover
price when you buy it back.

00:45:01.810 --> 00:45:05.082 align:middle line:84%
And so there are
these norms of people

00:45:05.082 --> 00:45:06.790 align:middle line:84%
would show up in the
bookstore at the end

00:45:06.790 --> 00:45:08.707 align:middle line:84%
of the semester willing
to buy back your books

00:45:08.707 --> 00:45:10.460 align:middle line:90%
at 50% of the cover price.

00:45:10.460 --> 00:45:12.950 align:middle line:84%
If you wanted to buy a used
book instead of a new book,

00:45:12.950 --> 00:45:17.790 align:middle line:84%
it would be sold at
75% of the cover price.

00:45:17.790 --> 00:45:23.280 align:middle line:84%
And from aggregate
numbers, roughly 30%

00:45:23.280 --> 00:45:26.510 align:middle line:84%
of the students in a course at
the time would buy a textbook.

00:45:26.510 --> 00:45:29.310 align:middle line:84%
About 20% would buy
a used textbook.

00:45:29.310 --> 00:45:31.380 align:middle line:84%
And then the other
50%, we hope, make

00:45:31.380 --> 00:45:34.530 align:middle line:84%
extensive use of the library
or some other resource

00:45:34.530 --> 00:45:36.725 align:middle line:84%
because they don't seem
to buy books at all.

00:45:36.725 --> 00:45:40.910 align:middle line:90%


00:45:40.910 --> 00:45:43.820 align:middle line:84%
The hazard rate
for revision seems

00:45:43.820 --> 00:45:46.550 align:middle line:84%
to peak in about the third
year, that many books

00:45:46.550 --> 00:45:49.110 align:middle line:84%
have a new version that
comes out every three years.

00:45:49.110 --> 00:45:51.380 align:middle line:84%
So if you're looking at
John Gruber's public finance

00:45:51.380 --> 00:45:54.750 align:middle line:84%
textbook, if it's been two
years since it's come out,

00:45:54.750 --> 00:45:57.650 align:middle line:84%
he's probably going to have
a new edition next year.

00:45:57.650 --> 00:45:59.930 align:middle line:84%
Once you've gone
a bit past three,

00:45:59.930 --> 00:46:02.510 align:middle line:84%
the probability that it gets
revised goes down and down

00:46:02.510 --> 00:46:06.380 align:middle line:84%
and down, because it's probably
the faculty member has just

00:46:06.380 --> 00:46:09.560 align:middle line:84%
gotten exhausted with the 11th
edition or the 12th or the 13th

00:46:09.560 --> 00:46:12.590 align:middle line:84%
and has decided they're not
making enough money off the new

00:46:12.590 --> 00:46:14.093 align:middle line:90%
editions to write the new ones.

00:46:14.093 --> 00:46:16.260 align:middle line:84%
They haven't got a new
person to take over the book.

00:46:16.260 --> 00:46:19.730 align:middle line:84%
But if the thing hasn't come
out and it's a few years late,

00:46:19.730 --> 00:46:21.235 align:middle line:90%
it's probably not coming out.

00:46:21.235 --> 00:46:24.460 align:middle line:90%


00:46:24.460 --> 00:46:27.760 align:middle line:84%
So papers, empirical approach
is a two-step strategy.

00:46:27.760 --> 00:46:29.320 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to estimate
the probability

00:46:29.320 --> 00:46:30.910 align:middle line:84%
that the book is
going to be revised

00:46:30.910 --> 00:46:32.830 align:middle line:90%
before the next semester.

00:46:32.830 --> 00:46:34.930 align:middle line:84%
And then I'm going
to see whether--

00:46:34.930 --> 00:46:37.720 align:middle line:84%
because prices don't
change, if you're a student

00:46:37.720 --> 00:46:40.060 align:middle line:84%
and you know a new book is
coming out next semester,

00:46:40.060 --> 00:46:41.938 align:middle line:84%
you shouldn't buy,
or you should be

00:46:41.938 --> 00:46:43.480 align:middle line:84%
willing to pay much
less for the book

00:46:43.480 --> 00:46:48.940 align:middle line:84%
because you know that the
cost is really $350, not $175,

00:46:48.940 --> 00:46:52.510 align:middle line:84%
so that, in some sense,
the price is much, much

00:46:52.510 --> 00:46:54.910 align:middle line:84%
higher in the semester
when a book is

00:46:54.910 --> 00:46:58.420 align:middle line:84%
about to be rendered obsolete
than it is in other semesters.

00:46:58.420 --> 00:47:01.450 align:middle line:84%
And so what we ought to
see is a drop in price,

00:47:01.450 --> 00:47:04.030 align:middle line:84%
a drop in demand
in semesters when

00:47:04.030 --> 00:47:05.655 align:middle line:84%
books are going to
be obsolete or when

00:47:05.655 --> 00:47:07.030 align:middle line:84%
people are going
to think there's

00:47:07.030 --> 00:47:10.840 align:middle line:84%
a higher probability that
they will become obsolete.

00:47:10.840 --> 00:47:14.690 align:middle line:84%
And if consumers are
rational and forward-looking,

00:47:14.690 --> 00:47:16.850 align:middle line:90%
we can see--

00:47:16.850 --> 00:47:19.400 align:middle line:84%
we can figure out-- if we
know the elasticity of demand

00:47:19.400 --> 00:47:22.340 align:middle line:84%
for books, we know how much
drop demand would go down

00:47:22.340 --> 00:47:23.872 align:middle line:90%
with such a price increase.

00:47:23.872 --> 00:47:25.580 align:middle line:84%
We can almost estimate
how many consumers

00:47:25.580 --> 00:47:27.330 align:middle line:84%
are rational and
forward-looking by seeing

00:47:27.330 --> 00:47:30.265 align:middle line:84%
how much does demand drop when
that new book is coming out.

00:47:30.265 --> 00:47:33.330 align:middle line:90%


00:47:33.330 --> 00:47:39.420 align:middle line:84%
So step 1 is a very
simple exercise.

00:47:39.420 --> 00:47:45.000 align:middle line:90%
This is just regress--

00:47:45.000 --> 00:47:47.760 align:middle line:84%
you just estimate a hazard
model where you just

00:47:47.760 --> 00:47:50.010 align:middle line:84%
have the probability
that the book is replaced

00:47:50.010 --> 00:47:53.910 align:middle line:84%
is a function of the age
of the book in semesters,

00:47:53.910 --> 00:47:57.570 align:middle line:84%
whether it's the biology or
psychology or economics book,

00:47:57.570 --> 00:48:03.603 align:middle line:84%
whether it's an introductory
textbook like for economics I

00:48:03.603 --> 00:48:05.520 align:middle line:84%
or whether it's an
intermediate micro textbook

00:48:05.520 --> 00:48:08.430 align:middle line:90%
or intermediate macro textbook.

00:48:08.430 --> 00:48:12.510 align:middle line:84%
And here's the graph they get
for the introductory books.

00:48:12.510 --> 00:48:15.300 align:middle line:84%
So you can see in
economics books,

00:48:15.300 --> 00:48:17.110 align:middle line:90%
your book is five semesters old.

00:48:17.110 --> 00:48:19.500 align:middle line:84%
There's, like, a 35% chance
your book will be rendered

00:48:19.500 --> 00:48:21.960 align:middle line:90%
obsolete one semester from now.

00:48:21.960 --> 00:48:25.122 align:middle line:84%
If your book is
six semesters old--

00:48:25.122 --> 00:48:26.950 align:middle line:84%
oh, actually, this
is six semesters old.

00:48:26.950 --> 00:48:30.365 align:middle line:84%
So there's actually
only an 8% chance

00:48:30.365 --> 00:48:32.990 align:middle line:84%
that your book is going to get
rendered obsolete next semester.

00:48:32.990 --> 00:48:34.150 align:middle line:90%
Or is it here?

00:48:34.150 --> 00:48:37.690 align:middle line:84%
Anyway, seven semesters
old, it's a 5% chance.

00:48:37.690 --> 00:48:40.420 align:middle line:84%
But there's this
big peak centered

00:48:40.420 --> 00:48:44.160 align:middle line:84%
around buying five-semester-old
books is a bad idea.

00:48:44.160 --> 00:48:47.770 align:middle line:84%
And the thing is less
consistent in some

00:48:47.770 --> 00:48:49.160 align:middle line:90%
of the other disciplines.

00:48:49.160 --> 00:48:52.150 align:middle line:84%
Some of the biology books
are being on a two-year cycle

00:48:52.150 --> 00:48:53.860 align:middle line:90%
instead of a three-year cycle.

00:48:53.860 --> 00:48:57.040 align:middle line:84%
Some of the psychology books
are on a 2-and-1/2 year cycle,

00:48:57.040 --> 00:48:57.900 align:middle line:90%
something like that.

00:48:57.900 --> 00:49:00.426 align:middle line:90%


00:49:00.426 --> 00:49:03.630 align:middle line:90%
And again, your book is--

00:49:03.630 --> 00:49:06.210 align:middle line:84%
your economics book is eight
years old, or any of your books

00:49:06.210 --> 00:49:08.310 align:middle line:84%
are eight years since
the edition has changed,

00:49:08.310 --> 00:49:10.080 align:middle line:84%
they're not going
to have a new one.

00:49:10.080 --> 00:49:11.660 align:middle line:84%
So that book is
very safe to buy.

00:49:11.660 --> 00:49:17.860 align:middle line:90%


00:49:17.860 --> 00:49:21.070 align:middle line:90%
How do we do demand?

00:49:21.070 --> 00:49:22.578 align:middle line:90%
So can I ask--

00:49:22.578 --> 00:49:24.620 align:middle line:84%
well, actually, some of
you haven't taken it yet.

00:49:24.620 --> 00:49:27.610 align:middle line:84%
So how much discrete choice
estimation is there left

00:49:27.610 --> 00:49:30.260 align:middle line:90%
in [14.]381?

00:49:30.260 --> 00:49:32.475 align:middle line:84%
Or has it all gone to
causal inference these days?

00:49:32.475 --> 00:49:35.558 align:middle line:90%


00:49:35.558 --> 00:49:36.850 align:middle line:90%
A lot of discrete choice or no?

00:49:36.850 --> 00:49:37.640 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Causal inference.

00:49:37.640 --> 00:49:39.000 align:middle line:84%
GLENN ELLISON: It's
all causal inference.

00:49:39.000 --> 00:49:41.390 align:middle line:84%
So is there any place where
people are doing logit models

00:49:41.390 --> 00:49:44.630 align:middle line:90%
and whatever?

00:49:44.630 --> 00:49:45.578 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:49:45.578 --> 00:49:47.210 align:middle line:90%
[LAUGHS]

00:49:47.210 --> 00:49:51.530 align:middle line:84%
OK, anyway, let me say, Tobias
has a full week of lectures

00:49:51.530 --> 00:49:53.547 align:middle line:84%
planned on discrete
choice estimation,

00:49:53.547 --> 00:49:55.380 align:middle line:84%
which obviously, a full
week is not so much.

00:49:55.380 --> 00:49:57.350 align:middle line:90%
There's much more in [14.]273.

00:49:57.350 --> 00:49:59.910 align:middle line:84%
But anyway, so it's week
four of this course,

00:49:59.910 --> 00:50:03.950 align:middle line:84%
Tobias is going to spend a week
on discrete choice estimation.

00:50:03.950 --> 00:50:06.770 align:middle line:84%
For now, let me just say
this is how everybody always

00:50:06.770 --> 00:50:08.920 align:middle line:84%
seems to do demand
estimation in IO these days.

00:50:08.920 --> 00:50:10.587 align:middle line:84%
There are a lot of
funny elements of it.

00:50:10.587 --> 00:50:13.850 align:middle line:84%
But so what we're doing here is
we have this model of consumers,

00:50:13.850 --> 00:50:15.482 align:middle line:84%
and we specify a
utility function

00:50:15.482 --> 00:50:17.690 align:middle line:84%
that they're going to get
from buying three objects--

00:50:17.690 --> 00:50:23.390 align:middle line:84%
a new book, a used book,
and no book at all.

00:50:23.390 --> 00:50:26.570 align:middle line:84%
And what utility do you
get if you buy a new book?

00:50:26.570 --> 00:50:29.330 align:middle line:84%
First, there's a
Xjt beta, which is

00:50:29.330 --> 00:50:31.250 align:middle line:84%
something on book
characteristics

00:50:31.250 --> 00:50:35.110 align:middle line:90%
times some coefficients.

00:50:35.110 --> 00:50:38.180 align:middle line:84%
Then there's the disutility
of paying for the book.

00:50:38.180 --> 00:50:41.940 align:middle line:84%
So alpha is your
price sensitivity.

00:50:41.940 --> 00:50:45.870 align:middle line:84%
And then we multiply
alpha by the net price

00:50:45.870 --> 00:50:47.490 align:middle line:90%
that you would pay.

00:50:47.490 --> 00:50:50.940 align:middle line:84%
The net price that you
would pay is the full price

00:50:50.940 --> 00:50:55.450 align:middle line:84%
minus a discount that
you apply to money that's

00:50:55.450 --> 00:50:59.010 align:middle line:84%
going to come to you
in the future times

00:50:59.010 --> 00:51:02.740 align:middle line:84%
the money you expect to get
back when you resell the book.

00:51:02.740 --> 00:51:07.120 align:middle line:84%
So this is if the book doesn't
die, I can sell it back.

00:51:07.120 --> 00:51:10.650 align:middle line:84%
And when I sell it back,
I get mu times Pjt,

00:51:10.650 --> 00:51:12.930 align:middle line:90%
where mu is maybe 50%.

00:51:12.930 --> 00:51:15.630 align:middle line:84%
And so this discount
factor delta

00:51:15.630 --> 00:51:19.140 align:middle line:84%
is both I'm getting the money
six months from now when I

00:51:19.140 --> 00:51:21.370 align:middle line:84%
sell it back instead
of getting it today.

00:51:21.370 --> 00:51:23.950 align:middle line:84%
And it's also, am I
thinking about it.

00:51:23.950 --> 00:51:27.000 align:middle line:84%
Like, you could imagine totally
myopic consumers are not

00:51:27.000 --> 00:51:29.790 align:middle line:84%
thinking about selling
it back, and they're just

00:51:29.790 --> 00:51:31.740 align:middle line:90%
treating delta as 0.

00:51:31.740 --> 00:51:34.740 align:middle line:84%
So they're just, Do I
want to pay $350 today,

00:51:34.740 --> 00:51:36.065 align:middle line:90%
not thinking about the future?

00:51:36.065 --> 00:51:40.374 align:middle line:90%


00:51:40.374 --> 00:51:43.640 align:middle line:84%
In order to explain the
data, there are always--

00:51:43.640 --> 00:51:45.690 align:middle line:84%
for some reason,
[INAUDIBLE] started

00:51:45.690 --> 00:51:48.720 align:middle line:90%
this use of the letter xi.

00:51:48.720 --> 00:51:52.230 align:middle line:84%
So this is the unobserved
quality of the book.

00:51:52.230 --> 00:51:56.040 align:middle line:84%
What is it that makes Pindyck
and Rubinfeld sell many more

00:51:56.040 --> 00:51:59.370 align:middle line:84%
copies than many other
intermediate micro textbooks?

00:51:59.370 --> 00:52:02.760 align:middle line:84%
The characteristics in terms
of how many pages long it is

00:52:02.760 --> 00:52:05.010 align:middle line:84%
and whether it's hardcover
and whether it has a CD-ROM

00:52:05.010 --> 00:52:06.460 align:middle line:90%
shrink-wrapped to it--

00:52:06.460 --> 00:52:08.400 align:middle line:90%
those, it's not very different.

00:52:08.400 --> 00:52:11.190 align:middle line:84%
But it must have
some unobserved--

00:52:11.190 --> 00:52:14.280 align:middle line:84%
or it may have some unobserved
quality that makes it a higher

00:52:14.280 --> 00:52:16.710 align:middle line:90%
fraction of students buy it.

00:52:16.710 --> 00:52:18.240 align:middle line:90%
And then you have this--

00:52:18.240 --> 00:52:20.430 align:middle line:84%
consumers have this
sort of a type.

00:52:20.430 --> 00:52:22.680 align:middle line:84%
In my previous lectures,
consumers always

00:52:22.680 --> 00:52:24.930 align:middle line:84%
had this type theta
that determined

00:52:24.930 --> 00:52:26.700 align:middle line:90%
like their taste for goods.

00:52:26.700 --> 00:52:29.530 align:middle line:84%
Here, consumers have a
three-dimensional type.

00:52:29.530 --> 00:52:33.630 align:middle line:84%
They have a three-dimensional
type epsilon

00:52:33.630 --> 00:52:42.790 align:middle line:84%
ijt new, epsilon ijt used,
and epsilon ijt none.

00:52:42.790 --> 00:52:44.380 align:middle line:84%
So I have this
three-dimensional type

00:52:44.380 --> 00:52:47.050 align:middle line:84%
describing how much I
like buying new books,

00:52:47.050 --> 00:52:48.910 align:middle line:84%
how much I like
buying used books,

00:52:48.910 --> 00:52:51.010 align:middle line:84%
and how much I like
buying no books at all

00:52:51.010 --> 00:52:52.930 align:middle line:90%
for this particular book.

00:52:52.930 --> 00:52:57.850 align:middle line:84%
And we model this as being
three independent extreme value

00:52:57.850 --> 00:53:00.100 align:middle line:90%
errors, typically.

00:53:00.100 --> 00:53:02.750 align:middle line:84%
So this would be my utility
for buying a new book.

00:53:02.750 --> 00:53:04.990 align:middle line:84%
Again, if my utility
for buying a used book

00:53:04.990 --> 00:53:09.130 align:middle line:84%
is going to be some function
of the book characteristics

00:53:09.130 --> 00:53:12.370 align:middle line:84%
plus some unobserved
quality of the used book

00:53:12.370 --> 00:53:14.080 align:middle line:84%
plus this
idiosyncratic reaction,

00:53:14.080 --> 00:53:17.220 align:middle line:84%
then I have an idiosyncratic
utility for buying no book.

00:53:17.220 --> 00:53:21.340 align:middle line:84%
And so what we model many
consumers doing is having these

00:53:21.340 --> 00:53:24.100 align:middle line:90%
three choices, and then based--

00:53:24.100 --> 00:53:25.930 align:middle line:84%
different people make
different choices

00:53:25.930 --> 00:53:30.630 align:middle line:84%
because different people
have different epsilons.

00:53:30.630 --> 00:53:32.310 align:middle line:84%
So we have this
utility specification,

00:53:32.310 --> 00:53:36.000 align:middle line:84%
but then given any
set of parameters--

00:53:36.000 --> 00:53:39.780 align:middle line:84%
beta, alpha, mu, et cetera--
and given assumptions

00:53:39.780 --> 00:53:42.960 align:middle line:84%
about the joint distributions
of these objects,

00:53:42.960 --> 00:53:44.460 align:middle line:84%
we're going to be
able to get demand

00:53:44.460 --> 00:53:46.300 align:middle line:84%
as a function of the
economic primitives.

00:53:46.300 --> 00:53:46.800 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:53:46.800 --> 00:53:48.967 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: So just do the
characteristics of the book

00:53:48.967 --> 00:53:53.560 align:middle line:84%
enter differently, and your user
is just yf or just another beta?

00:53:53.560 --> 00:53:55.440 align:middle line:84%
GLENN ELLISON: Yeah,
no, so it potentially

00:53:55.440 --> 00:53:58.020 align:middle line:84%
could be xjt times
beta, or it could

00:53:58.020 --> 00:53:59.970 align:middle line:90%
be some other function of xjt.

00:53:59.970 --> 00:54:02.940 align:middle line:84%
In their estimation,
it turns out it's--

00:54:02.940 --> 00:54:06.030 align:middle line:84%
under their estimation
assumptions, it doesn't matter.

00:54:06.030 --> 00:54:09.250 align:middle line:84%
In some sense, any function
here, the estimation would work.

00:54:09.250 --> 00:54:11.185 align:middle line:84%
So it's like we
estimate this robustly.

00:54:11.185 --> 00:54:13.685 align:middle line:84%
We don't need to assume this
equals this, and so they don't.

00:54:13.685 --> 00:54:17.370 align:middle line:90%


00:54:17.370 --> 00:54:22.410 align:middle line:84%
So theorem-- and this is
the magic of logit models.

00:54:22.410 --> 00:54:24.223 align:middle line:90%
The magic of logit models--

00:54:24.223 --> 00:54:26.640 align:middle line:84%
and, you know, this may seem
skeptical at first-- is like,

00:54:26.640 --> 00:54:29.970 align:middle line:84%
basically, you have
these quantities,

00:54:29.970 --> 00:54:32.040 align:middle line:84%
and then you take the
log of the quantity

00:54:32.040 --> 00:54:36.240 align:middle line:84%
and you subtract something, and
you have converted from quantity

00:54:36.240 --> 00:54:38.670 align:middle line:90%
space to utility space.

00:54:38.670 --> 00:54:40.890 align:middle line:90%
And again, Tobias explain more--

00:54:40.890 --> 00:54:43.300 align:middle line:84%
make this stronger argument
for why that's not--

00:54:43.300 --> 00:54:44.830 align:middle line:90%
doesn't seem so crazy.

00:54:44.830 --> 00:54:47.820 align:middle line:84%
Maybe it is crazy, but
people do it all the time.

00:54:47.820 --> 00:54:51.120 align:middle line:84%
So anyway, the theorem is,
if the epsilons are i.i.d.

00:54:51.120 --> 00:54:55.920 align:middle line:84%
across consumers and products,
and each of those epsilons

00:54:55.920 --> 00:54:58.890 align:middle line:84%
has a type 1 extreme
value distribution,

00:54:58.890 --> 00:55:03.510 align:middle line:84%
then the log of the ratio
between the number of people

00:55:03.510 --> 00:55:06.930 align:middle line:84%
buying new books and the
people buying no books

00:55:06.930 --> 00:55:13.060 align:middle line:90%
is that utility specification.

00:55:13.060 --> 00:55:16.210 align:middle line:84%
So it is book
characteristics times effect

00:55:16.210 --> 00:55:19.240 align:middle line:84%
of book characteristics on
utility minus alpha times

00:55:19.240 --> 00:55:23.170 align:middle line:84%
the price, plus alpha delta
mu times the probability

00:55:23.170 --> 00:55:25.210 align:middle line:84%
that it doesn't die
times the price,

00:55:25.210 --> 00:55:29.770 align:middle line:84%
plus this thing that was the
unobserved product quality

00:55:29.770 --> 00:55:34.900 align:middle line:84%
becomes the error term if
you're regressing new book

00:55:34.900 --> 00:55:39.560 align:middle line:84%
sales on book
characteristics and prices.

00:55:39.560 --> 00:55:42.950 align:middle line:84%
So we make this functional
form transformation,

00:55:42.950 --> 00:55:46.760 align:middle line:84%
and we get to this thing that we
could estimate these parameters,

00:55:46.760 --> 00:55:50.400 align:middle line:84%
the utility function, just
by running a regression.

00:55:50.400 --> 00:55:54.565 align:middle line:84%
So we're going to regress
log of the share--

00:55:54.565 --> 00:55:55.940 align:middle line:84%
log of the fraction
of people who

00:55:55.940 --> 00:56:01.010 align:middle line:84%
buy the book on book
characteristics,

00:56:01.010 --> 00:56:03.730 align:middle line:84%
prices, probability
of death, and so on.

00:56:03.730 --> 00:56:09.820 align:middle line:90%


00:56:09.820 --> 00:56:12.430 align:middle line:84%
One thing that this
specification points out

00:56:12.430 --> 00:56:16.242 align:middle line:84%
is, the coefficient on
the book characteristics

00:56:16.242 --> 00:56:17.950 align:middle line:84%
are going to tell us
how much people like

00:56:17.950 --> 00:56:20.773 align:middle line:90%
big books versus small books.

00:56:20.773 --> 00:56:23.440 align:middle line:84%
Coefficient on price is going to
tell us how much people dislike

00:56:23.440 --> 00:56:26.350 align:middle line:90%
buying books.

00:56:26.350 --> 00:56:29.290 align:middle line:84%
The coefficient
on the probability

00:56:29.290 --> 00:56:35.080 align:middle line:84%
of survival times the price is
going to give us alpha times

00:56:35.080 --> 00:56:36.550 align:middle line:90%
delta times mu.

00:56:36.550 --> 00:56:40.390 align:middle line:84%
So delta and mu are not
separately identified.

00:56:40.390 --> 00:56:43.690 align:middle line:84%
So if I said that delta was
the key parameter of are people

00:56:43.690 --> 00:56:46.300 align:middle line:84%
forward-looking, the
mu was how much money

00:56:46.300 --> 00:56:48.880 align:middle line:84%
do they expect to get back
when they resell the book,

00:56:48.880 --> 00:56:51.400 align:middle line:90%
if they're allowed to resell it.

00:56:51.400 --> 00:56:53.630 align:middle line:84%
We can't separately
identify this product.

00:56:53.630 --> 00:56:56.580 align:middle line:84%
All we can do is estimate those
two things multiplied together.

00:56:56.580 --> 00:57:03.600 align:middle line:90%


00:57:03.600 --> 00:57:06.270 align:middle line:84%
So another observation,
we're going

00:57:06.270 --> 00:57:08.578 align:middle line:90%
to need an instrument for price.

00:57:08.578 --> 00:57:10.620 align:middle line:84%
Why are we going to need
an instrument for price?

00:57:10.620 --> 00:57:12.245 align:middle line:84%
We always need an
instrument for price.

00:57:12.245 --> 00:57:17.730 align:middle line:84%
But if the firm has a book that
has a high unobserved quality,

00:57:17.730 --> 00:57:20.190 align:middle line:84%
this is a book that
many people like--

00:57:20.190 --> 00:57:23.220 align:middle line:84%
the same reason that just
like when demand is high,

00:57:23.220 --> 00:57:25.420 align:middle line:84%
price is high, to the
extent that's true.

00:57:25.420 --> 00:57:29.790 align:middle line:84%
But if the book has
an epsilon new that

00:57:29.790 --> 00:57:33.600 align:middle line:84%
makes the firm want
to set a high price,

00:57:33.600 --> 00:57:37.390 align:middle line:84%
then price is going to be
correlated with this error term,

00:57:37.390 --> 00:57:39.000 align:middle line:90%
so price is endogenous.

00:57:39.000 --> 00:57:42.870 align:middle line:84%
So we need an
instrument for price.

00:57:42.870 --> 00:57:44.360 align:middle line:90%
What do they do?

00:57:44.360 --> 00:57:45.840 align:middle line:84%
The instruments
they use for price

00:57:45.840 --> 00:57:48.340 align:middle line:84%
are having a
nonprofit publisher.

00:57:48.340 --> 00:57:50.430 align:middle line:84%
So, for instance,
Tirole's textbook

00:57:50.430 --> 00:57:52.890 align:middle line:84%
is published by MIT
Press rather than

00:57:52.890 --> 00:57:55.770 align:middle line:90%
by Macmillan or somebody else.

00:57:55.770 --> 00:57:59.610 align:middle line:84%
Tirole's book is cheaper
than many other books

00:57:59.610 --> 00:58:04.540 align:middle line:84%
because nonprofit publishers
tend to set lower prices.

00:58:04.540 --> 00:58:07.330 align:middle line:84%
Other instruments
they use are, What

00:58:07.330 --> 00:58:09.760 align:middle line:84%
fraction of your
close competitors

00:58:09.760 --> 00:58:11.930 align:middle line:84%
are published by
nonprofit publishers?

00:58:11.930 --> 00:58:15.190 align:middle line:84%
So if intermediate macro
books, a lot of them

00:58:15.190 --> 00:58:17.050 align:middle line:84%
are sold by
nonprofit publishers,

00:58:17.050 --> 00:58:18.730 align:middle line:84%
then maybe the
for-profit publishers

00:58:18.730 --> 00:58:21.340 align:middle line:90%
also have to set lower prices.

00:58:21.340 --> 00:58:23.800 align:middle line:84%
They also have a
measure, which is what's

00:58:23.800 --> 00:58:25.250 align:middle line:90%
the concentration of ownership?

00:58:25.250 --> 00:58:27.040 align:middle line:84%
Are there 10 different
major publishers

00:58:27.040 --> 00:58:30.700 align:middle line:84%
who have intermediate
machine learning books,

00:58:30.700 --> 00:58:33.250 align:middle line:84%
or are there only
two such publishers

00:58:33.250 --> 00:58:34.500 align:middle line:90%
who have dominated the market?

00:58:34.500 --> 00:58:38.910 align:middle line:90%


00:58:38.910 --> 00:58:43.160 align:middle line:84%
Anyway, so this is basically
the estimating equation.

00:58:43.160 --> 00:58:45.018 align:middle line:90%
So it's just an IV regression.

00:58:45.018 --> 00:58:47.560 align:middle line:84%
And it's an IV regression that,
under the assumptions they've

00:58:47.560 --> 00:58:49.990 align:middle line:84%
made, should let us
estimate delta mu, which

00:58:49.990 --> 00:58:50.910 align:middle line:90%
is what we care about.

00:58:50.910 --> 00:58:54.070 align:middle line:90%


00:58:54.070 --> 00:58:55.530 align:middle line:90%
And what do they find?

00:58:55.530 --> 00:58:58.670 align:middle line:90%


00:58:58.670 --> 00:59:03.600 align:middle line:84%
So they define lambda
equals delta times mu.

00:59:03.600 --> 00:59:05.310 align:middle line:90%
What did we think mu is?

00:59:05.310 --> 00:59:08.460 align:middle line:84%
We think that mu, you've got
to be able to get at least--

00:59:08.460 --> 00:59:11.170 align:middle line:84%
normally, you'd get 50%
back at the bookstore.

00:59:11.170 --> 00:59:13.170 align:middle line:84%
If you're going to sell
it back to your friends,

00:59:13.170 --> 00:59:15.930 align:middle line:84%
maybe you could go to your--
well, not your closest friends--

00:59:15.930 --> 00:59:18.738 align:middle line:84%
and say, I'll sell my
book to you for only 75%

00:59:18.738 --> 00:59:20.280 align:middle line:84%
of the cover price,
because if people

00:59:20.280 --> 00:59:22.860 align:middle line:84%
want to go to the
campus bookstore,

00:59:22.860 --> 00:59:25.248 align:middle line:84%
they can buy the used book
at 75% of the cover price.

00:59:25.248 --> 00:59:27.540 align:middle line:84%
You're not going be able to
sell it for more than that.

00:59:27.540 --> 00:59:29.640 align:middle line:84%
Just posting a sign
in the dorm saying,

00:59:29.640 --> 00:59:31.740 align:middle line:90%
I've got a used copy to sell.

00:59:31.740 --> 00:59:36.240 align:middle line:84%
But so if we think mu
is between 0.4 and 0.75,

00:59:36.240 --> 00:59:40.980 align:middle line:84%
they estimate is-- they
estimate lambda is 0.61.

00:59:40.980 --> 00:59:44.460 align:middle line:84%
And so if you estimate
that lambda is 0.61

00:59:44.460 --> 00:59:48.390 align:middle line:84%
and you think that
mu is in this range,

00:59:48.390 --> 00:59:50.790 align:middle line:84%
that's got to tell you
that mu is not 0.4.

00:59:50.790 --> 00:59:55.030 align:middle line:84%
Mu is bigger than 0.4, and
delta is pretty close to 1.

00:59:55.030 --> 00:59:58.630 align:middle line:84%
So the estimates are
indicating that consumers

00:59:58.630 --> 01:00:02.980 align:middle line:84%
must be fairly close to
all being forward-looking

01:00:02.980 --> 01:00:07.240 align:middle line:84%
and putting a very large weight
on that revision probability

01:00:07.240 --> 01:00:11.620 align:middle line:84%
because they're reacting
a great deal to books

01:00:11.620 --> 01:00:13.280 align:middle line:90%
that are about to die.

01:00:13.280 --> 01:00:15.520 align:middle line:84%
And they could only
react that much

01:00:15.520 --> 01:00:19.900 align:middle line:84%
to books that are about
to die if they recognize

01:00:19.900 --> 01:00:22.810 align:middle line:84%
they're going to die, or there's
a high probability of death

01:00:22.810 --> 01:00:24.953 align:middle line:90%
and put a lot of weight on that.

01:00:24.953 --> 01:00:26.870 align:middle line:84%
So in some sense, we're
estimating two things.

01:00:26.870 --> 01:00:29.420 align:middle line:90%
We get this--

01:00:29.420 --> 01:00:33.430 align:middle line:90%
I have alpha and alpha delta mu.

01:00:33.430 --> 01:00:36.470 align:middle line:84%
So the coefficient on
the probability of death

01:00:36.470 --> 01:00:40.680 align:middle line:84%
is giving me this, the
coefficient on the price

01:00:40.680 --> 01:00:43.035 align:middle line:84%
is giving me this, and
so then I divide them,

01:00:43.035 --> 01:00:44.660 align:middle line:84%
and that's where I
get the lambda from.

01:00:44.660 --> 01:00:53.560 align:middle line:90%


01:00:53.560 --> 01:00:55.125 align:middle line:90%
Any questions on that?

01:00:55.125 --> 01:00:59.290 align:middle line:90%


01:00:59.290 --> 01:01:01.890 align:middle line:84%
So, obviously, as with
any empirical paper,

01:01:01.890 --> 01:01:05.550 align:middle line:90%
I can think of lots of concerns.

01:01:05.550 --> 01:01:08.010 align:middle line:84%
And I think one of the hurdles
to getting research done

01:01:08.010 --> 01:01:09.210 align:middle line:84%
is you just-- there
are always going

01:01:09.210 --> 01:01:10.502 align:middle line:90%
to be problems with your paper.

01:01:10.502 --> 01:01:12.638 align:middle line:84%
You just go ahead
and write them.

01:01:12.638 --> 01:01:14.430 align:middle line:84%
In our profession, the
people who get ahead

01:01:14.430 --> 01:01:15.630 align:middle line:84%
are the people who
write the papers,

01:01:15.630 --> 01:01:17.172 align:middle line:84%
and then other people
criticize them.

01:01:17.172 --> 01:01:18.090 align:middle line:90%
That's OK.

01:01:18.090 --> 01:01:20.250 align:middle line:84%
Just writing the
papers and having

01:01:20.250 --> 01:01:21.750 align:middle line:84%
people talk about
your paper is what

01:01:21.750 --> 01:01:23.500 align:middle line:84%
you have to do to get
ahead in this world.

01:01:23.500 --> 01:01:25.800 align:middle line:84%
And so you write the
paper, there may be flaws,

01:01:25.800 --> 01:01:27.400 align:middle line:84%
there are things
people can criticize.

01:01:27.400 --> 01:01:30.550 align:middle line:84%
But as with any paper, there can
be lots of things to criticize,

01:01:30.550 --> 01:01:33.000 align:middle line:84%
and that's OK as long
as you get it out there.

01:01:33.000 --> 01:01:36.090 align:middle line:84%
So what can I criticize
about this one?

01:01:36.090 --> 01:01:39.100 align:middle line:84%
They have no data on
the non-bookstore sales.

01:01:39.100 --> 01:01:41.440 align:middle line:84%
They have very good data
on the bookstore sales

01:01:41.440 --> 01:01:43.900 align:middle line:84%
they get through the
bookstores themselves.

01:01:43.900 --> 01:01:46.832 align:middle line:84%
They really don't know
much about the used market.

01:01:46.832 --> 01:01:49.290 align:middle line:84%
They have a couple different
treatments of the used market,

01:01:49.290 --> 01:01:53.460 align:middle line:84%
but, in some sense, they're kind
of making up the used book sales

01:01:53.460 --> 01:01:57.940 align:middle line:90%
and just going with that.

01:01:57.940 --> 01:02:00.220 align:middle line:90%
Instruments are important.

01:02:00.220 --> 01:02:04.150 align:middle line:84%
I think the non-profit publisher
instrument is a good instrument.

01:02:04.150 --> 01:02:06.880 align:middle line:84%
But let's suppose you
haven't fully instrumented

01:02:06.880 --> 01:02:10.570 align:middle line:90%
for this thing, the lambda is--

01:02:10.570 --> 01:02:13.450 align:middle line:84%
lambda hat is going to
be like alpha delta mu

01:02:13.450 --> 01:02:16.120 align:middle line:90%
hat over alpha hat.

01:02:16.120 --> 01:02:18.760 align:middle line:84%
If the alpha hat ends
up biased toward zero,

01:02:18.760 --> 01:02:21.050 align:middle line:84%
you're going to be
inflating the lambda hat.

01:02:21.050 --> 01:02:24.820 align:middle line:84%
So imperfect job of
instrumenting for alpha

01:02:24.820 --> 01:02:27.500 align:middle line:84%
is going to make you think that
lambda is bigger than it is.

01:02:27.500 --> 01:02:29.590 align:middle line:84%
You worry, could that be
part of what's going on?

01:02:29.590 --> 01:02:32.260 align:middle line:90%


01:02:32.260 --> 01:02:35.990 align:middle line:84%
Distribution of errors--
again, we have that theorem.

01:02:35.990 --> 01:02:38.530 align:middle line:90%
If the epsilons are i.i.d.

01:02:38.530 --> 01:02:42.310 align:middle line:84%
extreme value-type errors,
then log of s over log of s

01:02:42.310 --> 01:02:44.562 align:middle line:90%
equals this.

01:02:44.562 --> 01:02:46.270 align:middle line:84%
There's a converse of
that theorem, which

01:02:46.270 --> 01:02:49.150 align:middle line:84%
says if the errors are not
i.i.d. extreme value type one,

01:02:49.150 --> 01:02:54.160 align:middle line:84%
then log of s new over s0
not equal to x times beta

01:02:54.160 --> 01:02:56.980 align:middle line:84%
minus alpha times p minus
alpha times p1 minus die.

01:02:56.980 --> 01:03:00.430 align:middle line:84%
So obviously, we're relying
on this functional form

01:03:00.430 --> 01:03:02.890 align:middle line:90%
identification in that sense.

01:03:02.890 --> 01:03:05.410 align:middle line:84%
We're also relying on
functional form identification

01:03:05.410 --> 01:03:08.860 align:middle line:84%
in the second sense, which is
the probability that a book will

01:03:08.860 --> 01:03:14.080 align:middle line:84%
die is a function of its
age and the field it's in

01:03:14.080 --> 01:03:16.060 align:middle line:90%
and other things.

01:03:16.060 --> 01:03:20.470 align:middle line:84%
So by using that probability
of death as an instrument

01:03:20.470 --> 01:03:21.790 align:middle line:90%
on the right side--

01:03:21.790 --> 01:03:25.540 align:middle line:84%
so the way the estimation
is actually done

01:03:25.540 --> 01:03:29.848 align:middle line:84%
is they put 1 minus whether
it actually died here

01:03:29.848 --> 01:03:32.140 align:middle line:84%
on the right side and then
use the probability of death

01:03:32.140 --> 01:03:34.790 align:middle line:84%
as an instrument for
whether it actually died.

01:03:34.790 --> 01:03:37.565 align:middle line:90%
But still, using that--

01:03:37.565 --> 01:03:39.190 align:middle line:84%
you're using as an
instrument something

01:03:39.190 --> 01:03:42.405 align:middle line:84%
that's a function of variables
that are all in your equation.

01:03:42.405 --> 01:03:43.780 align:middle line:84%
And so it has to
be that somehow,

01:03:43.780 --> 01:03:46.300 align:middle line:84%
that nonlinear
function of semesters

01:03:46.300 --> 01:03:49.480 align:middle line:84%
and whatever is uncorrelated
with the errors in the demand

01:03:49.480 --> 01:03:52.900 align:middle line:84%
equation, even though you put
the variables themselves also

01:03:52.900 --> 01:03:55.420 align:middle line:90%
on the right side just linearly.

01:03:55.420 --> 01:03:58.360 align:middle line:84%
So there's a use of functional
forms to identify that.

01:03:58.360 --> 01:04:00.490 align:middle line:84%
I should say, one
nice thing that they

01:04:00.490 --> 01:04:03.970 align:middle line:84%
did at the back of the paper to
try to make it more convincing

01:04:03.970 --> 01:04:07.940 align:middle line:84%
is this paper was written before
books were sold on Amazon.

01:04:07.940 --> 01:04:09.850 align:middle line:84%
They then got some
more recent data

01:04:09.850 --> 01:04:12.020 align:middle line:90%
of used books sold on Amazon.

01:04:12.020 --> 01:04:15.430 align:middle line:84%
And they also find this
effect there on the price side

01:04:15.430 --> 01:04:17.560 align:middle line:84%
that if you look at,
at what price can you

01:04:17.560 --> 01:04:21.760 align:middle line:84%
sell your used book on Amazon,
it goes down dramatically

01:04:21.760 --> 01:04:23.800 align:middle line:90%
in semester 5.

01:04:23.800 --> 01:04:28.330 align:middle line:84%
And sometimes, you go on Amazon,
and you see the 12th edition is

01:04:28.330 --> 01:04:30.760 align:middle line:84%
already available, not
yet available to buy,

01:04:30.760 --> 01:04:31.930 align:middle line:90%
but it's there.

01:04:31.930 --> 01:04:33.280 align:middle line:90%
I own the 11th--

01:04:33.280 --> 01:04:35.060 align:middle line:84%
I'm being asked to
buy the 11th edition.

01:04:35.060 --> 01:04:39.800 align:middle line:84%
The 12th edition is listed on
Amazon as available October 15.

01:04:39.800 --> 01:04:42.670 align:middle line:84%
In that situation,
price is really--

01:04:42.670 --> 01:04:44.820 align:middle line:84%
price of used books
really, really drop.

01:04:44.820 --> 01:04:51.790 align:middle line:90%


01:04:51.790 --> 01:04:53.715 align:middle line:84%
Any further questions
on this paper?

01:04:53.715 --> 01:04:59.910 align:middle line:90%


01:04:59.910 --> 01:05:02.995 align:middle line:84%
So let me go to final paper--
and again, this is a--

01:05:02.995 --> 01:05:04.990 align:middle line:84%
the final paper I thought
I'd talk about is--

01:05:04.990 --> 01:05:06.290 align:middle line:90%
it's 10 years old.

01:05:06.290 --> 01:05:07.130 align:middle line:90%
It's 10 years old.

01:05:07.130 --> 01:05:09.170 align:middle line:84%
It almost seems--
or 12 years old.

01:05:09.170 --> 01:05:12.010 align:middle line:84%
It almost seems more topical
than it did when it was written.

01:05:12.010 --> 01:05:14.620 align:middle line:90%
So this is about media slant.

01:05:14.620 --> 01:05:23.290 align:middle line:84%
This one, the question is, why
do we see biased news media?

01:05:23.290 --> 01:05:25.960 align:middle line:84%
One reason why you might
think we see biased news media

01:05:25.960 --> 01:05:29.590 align:middle line:84%
is maybe Rupert Murdoch
is very conservative

01:05:29.590 --> 01:05:33.410 align:middle line:84%
and wants to spread conservative
values across the United States.

01:05:33.410 --> 01:05:38.620 align:middle line:84%
Or maybe Newsmax is secretly
controlled by Russia,

01:05:38.620 --> 01:05:41.110 align:middle line:84%
and they're out to display
misinformation that will

01:05:41.110 --> 01:05:43.220 align:middle line:90%
destroy American democracy.

01:05:43.220 --> 01:05:47.200 align:middle line:84%
But another explanation would
simply be that running Fox News

01:05:47.200 --> 01:05:48.850 align:middle line:84%
and getting people
angry about things

01:05:48.850 --> 01:05:50.417 align:middle line:84%
is a very, very
profitable business.

01:05:50.417 --> 01:05:51.250 align:middle line:90%
And you don't even--

01:05:51.250 --> 01:05:52.833 align:middle line:84%
Rupert Murdoch doesn't
even have to be

01:05:52.833 --> 01:05:55.030 align:middle line:84%
conservative to want to
do this because he's just

01:05:55.030 --> 01:05:58.345 align:middle line:84%
making lots and lots of money
because people watch Fox News.

01:05:58.345 --> 01:06:01.542 align:middle line:90%


01:06:01.542 --> 01:06:03.500 align:middle line:84%
In this case, I said in
Chevalier and Goolsbee,

01:06:03.500 --> 01:06:06.080 align:middle line:84%
it was like there was a public
policy question about books

01:06:06.080 --> 01:06:08.330 align:middle line:84%
and why they're so expensive
and why they get revised.

01:06:08.330 --> 01:06:10.695 align:middle line:84%
But then there's an
understanding the theory better.

01:06:10.695 --> 01:06:13.070 align:middle line:84%
Here, I think it's less of an
understanding of the theory

01:06:13.070 --> 01:06:15.170 align:middle line:84%
better than just recognizing
the theory, which

01:06:15.170 --> 01:06:17.300 align:middle line:84%
is the theory is the
optimal product quality

01:06:17.300 --> 01:06:20.810 align:middle line:84%
theorem from monopoly pricing,
that we think monopolists ought

01:06:20.810 --> 01:06:22.970 align:middle line:84%
to choose the quality
of their product

01:06:22.970 --> 01:06:25.170 align:middle line:84%
that is optimal to
maximize profits.

01:06:25.170 --> 01:06:27.680 align:middle line:84%
And so the question here is,
does that optimal quality

01:06:27.680 --> 01:06:30.560 align:middle line:84%
theorem apply to help
us understand Fox News,

01:06:30.560 --> 01:06:34.820 align:middle line:84%
and does it look like Fox
News is profit maximizing,

01:06:34.820 --> 01:06:37.850 align:middle line:84%
and do we understand things
are profit maximizing?

01:06:37.850 --> 01:06:40.890 align:middle line:84%
Now, turns out this paper
is not about Fox News.

01:06:40.890 --> 01:06:42.560 align:middle line:90%
This is about newspapers.

01:06:42.560 --> 01:06:47.340 align:middle line:90%
And again, things do age.

01:06:47.340 --> 01:06:49.230 align:middle line:84%
There was a time in
the United States

01:06:49.230 --> 01:06:52.500 align:middle line:84%
not so long ago when
people read newspapers.

01:06:52.500 --> 01:06:55.650 align:middle line:84%
Newspapers were things printed
on physical paper, delivered

01:06:55.650 --> 01:06:57.772 align:middle line:90%
to people's houses by carriers.

01:06:57.772 --> 01:07:00.480 align:middle line:84%
And then you would get up in the
morning, pick up your newspaper,

01:07:00.480 --> 01:07:04.380 align:middle line:84%
and read it while drinking your
coffee and eating breakfast.

01:07:04.380 --> 01:07:07.540 align:middle line:84%
There were many, many local
newspapers in the United States.

01:07:07.540 --> 01:07:09.580 align:middle line:84%
Every city had
multiple newspapers.

01:07:09.580 --> 01:07:11.940 align:middle line:84%
So in Boston, instead of
just having the Boston Globe,

01:07:11.940 --> 01:07:14.232 align:middle line:84%
we had the Boston Globe and
something called the Boston

01:07:14.232 --> 01:07:14.820 align:middle line:90%
Herald.

01:07:14.820 --> 01:07:16.530 align:middle line:84%
Boston Herald was
smaller and easier

01:07:16.530 --> 01:07:18.930 align:middle line:84%
to read while riding
on the subway.

01:07:18.930 --> 01:07:22.090 align:middle line:84%
But many towns across the
United States had newspapers.

01:07:22.090 --> 01:07:24.330 align:middle line:84%
Those newspapers would
convey both local news

01:07:24.330 --> 01:07:25.520 align:middle line:90%
and national news.

01:07:25.520 --> 01:07:30.000 align:middle line:90%


01:07:30.000 --> 01:07:35.650 align:middle line:84%
So let's see, motivation--
so anyway, media slant

01:07:35.650 --> 01:07:37.640 align:middle line:90%
remains a very big topic.

01:07:37.640 --> 01:07:38.680 align:middle line:90%
I should say that--

01:07:38.680 --> 01:07:41.390 align:middle line:84%
I mentioned Fox News, I think
another very interesting topic,

01:07:41.390 --> 01:07:44.750 align:middle line:84%
there's a company flying more
under the radar, Sinclair Media.

01:07:44.750 --> 01:07:48.350 align:middle line:84%
Sinclair Media is basically the
Fox News of local television.

01:07:48.350 --> 01:07:52.600 align:middle line:84%
So Sinclair has bought hundreds
and hundreds of local stations

01:07:52.600 --> 01:07:55.780 align:middle line:84%
across the United States and
made the 10:00 and 11:00 news

01:07:55.780 --> 01:07:59.220 align:middle line:84%
that you receive free over
the air more Fox News-like.

01:07:59.220 --> 01:08:02.200 align:middle line:90%


01:08:02.200 --> 01:08:04.700 align:middle line:84%
Anyway, what
Gentzkow and Shapiro

01:08:04.700 --> 01:08:07.060 align:middle line:84%
were trying to do
is ideally, they

01:08:07.060 --> 01:08:10.510 align:middle line:84%
would like to have this two-step
investigation of understanding

01:08:10.510 --> 01:08:12.970 align:middle line:90%
motivations for biased news.

01:08:12.970 --> 01:08:17.600 align:middle line:84%
Step one is estimate the slant
that would be profit maximizing.

01:08:17.600 --> 01:08:21.490 align:middle line:84%
And then step two is compare
the actual and profit-maximizing

01:08:21.490 --> 01:08:24.199 align:middle line:90%
slant and see whether there--

01:08:24.199 --> 01:08:26.103 align:middle line:84%
are there differences
between the two?

01:08:26.103 --> 01:08:27.520 align:middle line:84%
If there are
differences, are they

01:08:27.520 --> 01:08:31.370 align:middle line:84%
related to the ideology of the
person who owns the newspaper?

01:08:31.370 --> 01:08:34.310 align:middle line:90%


01:08:34.310 --> 01:08:39.649 align:middle line:84%
I think the paper falls a little
short of the self-description,

01:08:39.649 --> 01:08:41.698 align:middle line:84%
or it's a little heroic
in the assumptions

01:08:41.698 --> 01:08:43.490 align:middle line:84%
that you need to think
that it's estimating

01:08:43.490 --> 01:08:45.710 align:middle line:90%
profit-maximizing slants.

01:08:45.710 --> 01:08:50.880 align:middle line:84%
But it has been very influential
for the questions it raised

01:08:50.880 --> 01:08:54.090 align:middle line:84%
and also, actually, for the ways
it's quantifying media bias.

01:08:54.090 --> 01:08:56.250 align:middle line:84%
And techniques
developed here get

01:08:56.250 --> 01:08:58.999 align:middle line:84%
used in many other
papers on media bias.

01:08:58.999 --> 01:09:02.210 align:middle line:90%


01:09:02.210 --> 01:09:03.859 align:middle line:90%
OK, so quantifying media slant--

01:09:03.859 --> 01:09:06.470 align:middle line:90%


01:09:06.470 --> 01:09:08.967 align:middle line:84%
this was really one of
the first papers to use--

01:09:08.967 --> 01:09:10.550 align:middle line:84%
you wouldn't even
these days call them

01:09:10.550 --> 01:09:12.680 align:middle line:84%
machine learning techniques,
but automated text

01:09:12.680 --> 01:09:15.590 align:middle line:90%
processing as a way to get data.

01:09:15.590 --> 01:09:18.500 align:middle line:84%
And so what they're trying to do
is they're trying to figure out,

01:09:18.500 --> 01:09:23.800 align:middle line:84%
is a newspaper right skewed
or is a newspaper left skewed?

01:09:23.800 --> 01:09:26.680 align:middle line:84%
How do you make a newspaper
skewed one way or the other?

01:09:26.680 --> 01:09:29.490 align:middle line:84%
Well, one thing you do is
just with the words you use.

01:09:29.490 --> 01:09:32.180 align:middle line:84%
And in the early 2000s,
they give examples like,

01:09:32.180 --> 01:09:35.569 align:middle line:84%
do you call something the
Iraq War or the War on Terror?

01:09:35.569 --> 01:09:37.760 align:middle line:84%
And generally, people
who favored the Iraq War

01:09:37.760 --> 01:09:39.130 align:middle line:90%
would call it the War on Terror.

01:09:39.130 --> 01:09:43.100 align:middle line:84%
People who opposed the War in
Iraq would call it the Iraq War.

01:09:43.100 --> 01:09:46.729 align:middle line:84%
Estate tax and death tax is
a very nice linguistic thing.

01:09:46.729 --> 01:09:50.240 align:middle line:84%
In the United States, if you
have less than $5 million

01:09:50.240 --> 01:09:53.720 align:middle line:84%
when you die, you can pass
it on to your heirs tax free.

01:09:53.720 --> 01:09:55.822 align:middle line:84%
If you have billions
of dollars, it

01:09:55.822 --> 01:09:58.280 align:middle line:84%
seems like you can often pass
it on to your heirs tax free,

01:09:58.280 --> 01:10:00.660 align:middle line:84%
never having paid the
capital gains taxes either.

01:10:00.660 --> 01:10:03.060 align:middle line:84%
But there are taxes on
the books that you're

01:10:03.060 --> 01:10:06.930 align:middle line:84%
supposed to pay when you
pass your billions of dollars

01:10:06.930 --> 01:10:09.600 align:middle line:90%
on to your children.

01:10:09.600 --> 01:10:13.530 align:middle line:84%
But anyway, the Republicans tend
to refer to it as the death tax,

01:10:13.530 --> 01:10:16.283 align:middle line:84%
because who wants to
pile on someone's misery

01:10:16.283 --> 01:10:17.700 align:middle line:84%
when someone has
died and tax them

01:10:17.700 --> 01:10:19.860 align:middle line:90%
for it, versus the estate tax.

01:10:19.860 --> 01:10:21.550 align:middle line:84%
Everyone can agree
on taxing estates.

01:10:21.550 --> 01:10:23.280 align:middle line:84%
If you have wealthy
people, estate--

01:10:23.280 --> 01:10:25.062 align:middle line:84%
wealthy estates
should pay taxes.

01:10:25.062 --> 01:10:26.520 align:middle line:84%
So there's a question
of what words

01:10:26.520 --> 01:10:28.210 align:middle line:90%
do you use in your newspaper?

01:10:28.210 --> 01:10:29.760 align:middle line:90%
But then much more--

01:10:29.760 --> 01:10:31.860 align:middle line:84%
if you look at what Fox
News does, some of it

01:10:31.860 --> 01:10:34.658 align:middle line:84%
may be outright lying about
voting machine tampering

01:10:34.658 --> 01:10:36.450 align:middle line:84%
or something like that
to get people angry.

01:10:36.450 --> 01:10:38.085 align:middle line:84%
But much more,
it's a question of,

01:10:38.085 --> 01:10:39.840 align:middle line:84%
do you have a lot
of news stories

01:10:39.840 --> 01:10:43.680 align:middle line:84%
about undocumented immigrants
assaulting and killing people?

01:10:43.680 --> 01:10:48.460 align:middle line:84%
Or do you have a lot
of stories about people

01:10:48.460 --> 01:10:51.160 align:middle line:84%
invading the US capitol
and beating police officers

01:10:51.160 --> 01:10:52.120 align:middle line:90%
or whatever?

01:10:52.120 --> 01:10:57.090 align:middle line:84%
So in some sense, the
extent to which you

01:10:57.090 --> 01:10:59.490 align:middle line:84%
talk about tax breaks
being given to corporations

01:10:59.490 --> 01:11:01.590 align:middle line:84%
or the extent you talk
about illegal immigrants

01:11:01.590 --> 01:11:04.980 align:middle line:84%
committing crimes is going to
be one way in which you can

01:11:04.980 --> 01:11:06.720 align:middle line:84%
slant your news
coverage and change

01:11:06.720 --> 01:11:09.330 align:middle line:84%
political opinions of the
people who live in the area

01:11:09.330 --> 01:11:11.070 align:middle line:90%
that you're serving.

01:11:11.070 --> 01:11:15.600 align:middle line:84%
And so what they try to do is--
the question is, how do you

01:11:15.600 --> 01:11:19.560 align:middle line:84%
pick out a Democratic from a
Republican issue or a Democratic

01:11:19.560 --> 01:11:21.785 align:middle line:84%
from a Republican talking
point or Democratic

01:11:21.785 --> 01:11:23.380 align:middle line:90%
from Republican phrasing?

01:11:23.380 --> 01:11:26.880 align:middle line:84%
And what they do is they
go through the full text

01:11:26.880 --> 01:11:29.723 align:middle line:84%
of all speeches
given on the floor

01:11:29.723 --> 01:11:31.140 align:middle line:84%
of the US House
of Representatives

01:11:31.140 --> 01:11:32.340 align:middle line:90%
and the House of Senate.

01:11:32.340 --> 01:11:34.710 align:middle line:84%
And they pick up two-
and three-word phrases

01:11:34.710 --> 01:11:38.190 align:middle line:84%
that are being used by
Democrats and Republicans.

01:11:38.190 --> 01:11:42.360 align:middle line:84%
And they actually classify
each congressperson as a

01:11:42.360 --> 01:11:45.090 align:middle line:84%
where they are on a
continuum spectrum

01:11:45.090 --> 01:11:49.050 align:middle line:84%
by what's the vote
share for George W.

01:11:49.050 --> 01:11:51.820 align:middle line:84%
Bush in their
congressional district.

01:11:51.820 --> 01:11:53.700 align:middle line:84%
And so they use this
for what is being

01:11:53.700 --> 01:11:55.570 align:middle line:84%
spoken by far left
people, what's being

01:11:55.570 --> 01:11:57.290 align:middle line:90%
spoken by far right people.

01:11:57.290 --> 01:11:58.990 align:middle line:90%
And so they identify basically--

01:11:58.990 --> 01:12:05.200 align:middle line:90%


01:12:05.200 --> 01:12:08.980 align:middle line:84%
they select 1,000 two- and
three-word phrases that are used

01:12:08.980 --> 01:12:11.260 align:middle line:84%
significantly different
frequencies by people

01:12:11.260 --> 01:12:13.960 align:middle line:84%
on the left versus the right
of the political spectrum.

01:12:13.960 --> 01:12:17.200 align:middle line:84%
And then for each
newspaper, they

01:12:17.200 --> 01:12:19.330 align:middle line:84%
regress the excess
frequency with which

01:12:19.330 --> 01:12:23.230 align:middle line:84%
it uses each phrase on that
phrase's Republican-ness.

01:12:23.230 --> 01:12:28.630 align:middle line:84%
And they define the slant of
the newspaper as the coefficient

01:12:28.630 --> 01:12:32.050 align:middle line:90%
on that variable.

01:12:32.050 --> 01:12:35.800 align:middle line:84%
So if it's really using
almost exclusively right

01:12:35.800 --> 01:12:38.500 align:middle line:84%
Republican phrases, it's going
to be a very high coefficient.

01:12:38.500 --> 01:12:41.000 align:middle line:84%
If it's using mostly
Democratic phrases--

01:12:41.000 --> 01:12:45.820 align:middle line:84%
so if the distribution
looks like this,

01:12:45.820 --> 01:12:47.882 align:middle line:84%
it's going to be a very
left-skewed newspaper.

01:12:47.882 --> 01:12:49.840 align:middle line:84%
If the distribution of
phrases looks like that,

01:12:49.840 --> 01:12:51.798 align:middle line:84%
it's going to be a very
right-skewed newspaper.

01:12:51.798 --> 01:12:55.230 align:middle line:84%
And they measure the skew by the
slope of those estimated lines.

01:12:55.230 --> 01:12:58.470 align:middle line:90%


01:12:58.470 --> 01:13:02.070 align:middle line:84%
Now, obviously, part of
that in these examples sound

01:13:02.070 --> 01:13:04.257 align:middle line:84%
like things where you're
doing media slant.

01:13:04.257 --> 01:13:06.840 align:middle line:84%
There are also going to be all
kinds of other things picked up

01:13:06.840 --> 01:13:07.340 align:middle line:90%
in this.

01:13:07.340 --> 01:13:10.290 align:middle line:84%
So, for instance,
some Congresspeople

01:13:10.290 --> 01:13:12.540 align:middle line:84%
talk about agriculture
policy, and some

01:13:12.540 --> 01:13:17.100 align:middle line:84%
talk about guns in their
urban neighborhoods.

01:13:17.100 --> 01:13:19.290 align:middle line:84%
Talking about agriculture
policy may just

01:13:19.290 --> 01:13:21.480 align:middle line:84%
mean that you're talking
about agricultural policy.

01:13:21.480 --> 01:13:24.720 align:middle line:84%
But generally, Republicans
tend to represent

01:13:24.720 --> 01:13:27.420 align:middle line:84%
agricultural states,
and so you may get corn

01:13:27.420 --> 01:13:30.960 align:middle line:84%
as being a Republican
phrase when it's not--

01:13:30.960 --> 01:13:36.810 align:middle line:84%
talking about corn prices is
not a political statement.

01:13:36.810 --> 01:13:39.190 align:middle line:84%
They didn't pick the phrases
to have political content.

01:13:39.190 --> 01:13:41.200 align:middle line:84%
They just picked
phrases automatically.

01:13:41.200 --> 01:13:44.340 align:middle line:84%
And so there's going to be some
contamination of political slant

01:13:44.340 --> 01:13:45.526 align:middle line:90%
with other differences.

01:13:45.526 --> 01:13:50.780 align:middle line:90%


01:13:50.780 --> 01:13:52.940 align:middle line:84%
Consumer demand,
again, is going to be

01:13:52.940 --> 01:13:55.190 align:middle line:90%
derived from utility function.

01:13:55.190 --> 01:14:01.010 align:middle line:84%
We assume that the utility
that consumer I in ZIP code Z

01:14:01.010 --> 01:14:04.520 align:middle line:84%
gets from buying newspaper
N is some function

01:14:04.520 --> 01:14:12.430 align:middle line:84%
of the average quality of the
newspaper minus disutility

01:14:12.430 --> 01:14:14.980 align:middle line:84%
that the consumer has from
consuming something that's

01:14:14.980 --> 01:14:17.700 align:middle line:90%
different from the ideal slant.

01:14:17.700 --> 01:14:21.450 align:middle line:84%
So Iz is the ideal slant
of all people in ZIP code

01:14:21.450 --> 01:14:25.570 align:middle line:84%
Z. yn is the actual
slant of newspaper n.

01:14:25.570 --> 01:14:28.990 align:middle line:84%
So as the slant of
the newspaper differs

01:14:28.990 --> 01:14:31.730 align:middle line:84%
from what's ideal for
people in that ZIP code,

01:14:31.730 --> 01:14:34.960 align:middle line:84%
utility of people in
that ZIP code goes down.

01:14:34.960 --> 01:14:36.900 align:middle line:84%
And then there's a
random error term.

01:14:36.900 --> 01:14:41.610 align:middle line:90%


01:14:41.610 --> 01:14:44.160 align:middle line:84%
They assume that the
utility-maximizing slant

01:14:44.160 --> 01:14:49.770 align:middle line:84%
in ZIP code z is just alpha
plus beta times rz, where

01:14:49.770 --> 01:14:53.580 align:middle line:84%
rz is the fraction of people in
ZIP code z who are Republican.

01:14:53.580 --> 01:14:58.900 align:middle line:84%
So it's a very specific
assumption in several ways.

01:14:58.900 --> 01:15:01.280 align:middle line:90%
So there's no heterogeneity.

01:15:01.280 --> 01:15:03.510 align:middle line:84%
All people in ZIP
code z have exactly

01:15:03.510 --> 01:15:06.790 align:middle line:90%
the same preferred slant.

01:15:06.790 --> 01:15:11.190 align:middle line:84%
And there is no error
in this equation.

01:15:11.190 --> 01:15:15.340 align:middle line:84%
All people in-- preferred
slant in ZIP code z

01:15:15.340 --> 01:15:18.250 align:middle line:84%
is this linear function of
the fraction Republican,

01:15:18.250 --> 01:15:20.560 align:middle line:84%
with no heterogeneity
across ZIP codes.

01:15:20.560 --> 01:15:23.825 align:middle line:84%
There are no ZIP codes
where a left slant is better

01:15:23.825 --> 01:15:26.325 align:middle line:84%
than you would think, given how
many Republicans live there.

01:15:26.325 --> 01:15:30.330 align:middle line:90%


01:15:30.330 --> 01:15:33.810 align:middle line:84%
Once again, they also assume
now that these epsilon izn's

01:15:33.810 --> 01:15:34.500 align:middle line:90%
are i.i.d.

01:15:34.500 --> 01:15:37.740 align:middle line:84%
extreme value, and then they
make this magical transformation

01:15:37.740 --> 01:15:40.470 align:middle line:84%
from sales of the
newspaper to utility

01:15:40.470 --> 01:15:42.280 align:middle line:90%
of the purchasing consumers.

01:15:42.280 --> 01:15:48.720 align:middle line:84%
And they get that the log of
the market share of purchasers

01:15:48.720 --> 01:15:53.430 align:middle line:84%
versus nonpurchasers is the
average utility minus gamma

01:15:53.430 --> 01:15:56.130 align:middle line:84%
times the difference
between the newspaper slant

01:15:56.130 --> 01:16:03.270 align:middle line:84%
and that linear function of the
fraction Republican in the ZIP

01:16:03.270 --> 01:16:04.050 align:middle line:90%
code.

01:16:04.050 --> 01:16:07.680 align:middle line:84%
And obviously, we can just
expand this quadratic.

01:16:07.680 --> 01:16:09.390 align:middle line:84%
And when you expand
this quadratic,

01:16:09.390 --> 01:16:11.593 align:middle line:84%
you're just going
to get things like--

01:16:11.593 --> 01:16:13.260 align:middle line:84%
you're going to get
y on the right side.

01:16:13.260 --> 01:16:14.760 align:middle line:90%
You're going to get y squared.

01:16:14.760 --> 01:16:16.468 align:middle line:84%
You're going to get
r squared, and you're

01:16:16.468 --> 01:16:17.730 align:middle line:90%
going to get y times r.

01:16:17.730 --> 01:16:19.647 align:middle line:84%
So you're just going to
have this thing that's

01:16:19.647 --> 01:16:23.610 align:middle line:84%
this right-hand side term that's
got y's and r's and y squareds

01:16:23.610 --> 01:16:26.800 align:middle line:90%
and r squareds and y times r.

01:16:26.800 --> 01:16:33.500 align:middle line:84%
And in this model, if
this model is correct,

01:16:33.500 --> 01:16:37.970 align:middle line:84%
the utility-maximizing
slant is alpha plus beta rz.

01:16:37.970 --> 01:16:42.910 align:middle line:84%
And we can estimate how the
utility-maximizing slant varies

01:16:42.910 --> 01:16:46.390 align:middle line:84%
with rz by just looking at the
coefficient of-- the coefficient

01:16:46.390 --> 01:16:50.403 align:middle line:90%
of yn times rz is going to be--

01:16:50.403 --> 01:16:55.420 align:middle line:90%


01:16:55.420 --> 01:17:01.510 align:middle line:84%
it's going to be gamma minus
2 times gamma times beta.

01:17:01.510 --> 01:17:04.605 align:middle line:84%
So anyway, we're
going to get that--

01:17:04.605 --> 01:17:08.160 align:middle line:84%
yeah, the coefficient
on yn times

01:17:08.160 --> 01:17:11.208 align:middle line:84%
rz is going to be minus
2 times gamma times beta.

01:17:11.208 --> 01:17:12.750 align:middle line:84%
And so we're going
to be estimating--

01:17:12.750 --> 01:17:15.750 align:middle line:84%
we're going to get gamma
times-- identify gamma times

01:17:15.750 --> 01:17:18.900 align:middle line:84%
beta by just looking at
the coefficient of this

01:17:18.900 --> 01:17:22.980 align:middle line:84%
when I regress log
of share of purchases

01:17:22.980 --> 01:17:26.410 align:middle line:84%
of the newspaper on the
right-hand side variables.

01:17:26.410 --> 01:17:32.340 align:middle line:90%


01:17:32.340 --> 01:17:35.160 align:middle line:90%
What did they find?

01:17:35.160 --> 01:17:37.290 align:middle line:84%
The clear finding
is that this ZIP

01:17:37.290 --> 01:17:44.980 align:middle line:84%
share Republican times slant has
a clear, positive, significant

01:17:44.980 --> 01:17:45.890 align:middle line:90%
coefficient.

01:17:45.890 --> 01:17:51.070 align:middle line:84%
So this is evidence
that the ideal slant

01:17:51.070 --> 01:17:56.140 align:middle line:84%
is to put more Republican
news in a Republican ZIP code

01:17:56.140 --> 01:18:00.160 align:middle line:90%
from a profit-maximizing sense.

01:18:00.160 --> 01:18:04.910 align:middle line:84%
Let me say that my
view on this is--

01:18:04.910 --> 01:18:06.470 align:middle line:84%
it's a very, very
strong assumption

01:18:06.470 --> 01:18:08.512 align:middle line:84%
to assume that there's a
profit-maximizing slant,

01:18:08.512 --> 01:18:11.480 align:middle line:84%
and it doesn't differ across
consumers within a ZIP code,

01:18:11.480 --> 01:18:14.900 align:middle line:84%
and it has no error
term across ZIP codes.

01:18:14.900 --> 01:18:18.473 align:middle line:84%
I find it convincing
evidence that in more

01:18:18.473 --> 01:18:20.390 align:middle line:84%
right-leaning areas, you
do better with a more

01:18:20.390 --> 01:18:22.950 align:middle line:90%
right-leaning newspaper.

01:18:22.950 --> 01:18:27.350 align:middle line:84%
The fact that this slant is
the profit-maximizing slant--

01:18:27.350 --> 01:18:31.020 align:middle line:84%
that relies on those no errors,
homogeneity assumptions.

01:18:31.020 --> 01:18:32.900 align:middle line:84%
So it takes pretty
heroic assumptions

01:18:32.900 --> 01:18:35.690 align:middle line:84%
to say that this really is
the profit-maximizing slant.

01:18:35.690 --> 01:18:37.760 align:middle line:84%
But I do think it's
pretty clear evidence

01:18:37.760 --> 01:18:40.580 align:middle line:84%
that right-slanted
news does sell better

01:18:40.580 --> 01:18:43.310 align:middle line:84%
in right-slanted areas,
which is fundamentally--

01:18:43.310 --> 01:18:46.940 align:middle line:84%
we're regressing log of q on
the share Republican times

01:18:46.940 --> 01:18:48.950 align:middle line:84%
slant interaction and
getting a positive effect,

01:18:48.950 --> 01:18:51.860 align:middle line:84%
saying in more Republican
areas, more right-slanted news

01:18:51.860 --> 01:18:52.945 align:middle line:90%
is selling more copies.

01:18:52.945 --> 01:18:58.940 align:middle line:90%


01:18:58.940 --> 01:19:05.760 align:middle line:84%
And then is slant
profit maximizing?

01:19:05.760 --> 01:19:08.100 align:middle line:90%
They show this graph.

01:19:08.100 --> 01:19:11.220 align:middle line:84%
And this graph is
supposed to show you,

01:19:11.220 --> 01:19:15.150 align:middle line:84%
are newspapers more
right slanted in more

01:19:15.150 --> 01:19:16.270 align:middle line:90%
Republican areas?

01:19:16.270 --> 01:19:19.290 align:middle line:84%
And the answer,
unsurprisingly, is yes.

01:19:19.290 --> 01:19:22.530 align:middle line:90%
So we get this positive slope.

01:19:22.530 --> 01:19:25.020 align:middle line:84%
The more Republican is the
area in which your newspaper is

01:19:25.020 --> 01:19:28.680 align:middle line:84%
being sold, the more
right you do make

01:19:28.680 --> 01:19:31.710 align:middle line:90%
the slant of your newspaper.

01:19:31.710 --> 01:19:35.170 align:middle line:84%
The demand estimates
aren't separately

01:19:35.170 --> 01:19:37.540 align:middle line:84%
identifying gamma and
beta, so it's a little hard

01:19:37.540 --> 01:19:40.450 align:middle line:84%
to say whether this slope
is the slope we would expect

01:19:40.450 --> 01:19:44.093 align:middle line:90%
given the profit maximization.

01:19:44.093 --> 01:19:45.510 align:middle line:84%
So then the final
thing they do is

01:19:45.510 --> 01:19:48.040 align:middle line:90%
they say, well, let's look at--

01:19:48.040 --> 01:19:52.330 align:middle line:84%
regress the slant on the
share Republican in the market

01:19:52.330 --> 01:19:58.443 align:middle line:84%
and then ownership fixed effects
to see whether it looks like--

01:19:58.443 --> 01:19:59.360 align:middle line:90%
they do this two ways.

01:19:59.360 --> 01:20:01.540 align:middle line:84%
One is they look at owner
political contributions

01:20:01.540 --> 01:20:04.100 align:middle line:84%
and put owner political
contributions on the right side.

01:20:04.100 --> 01:20:06.050 align:middle line:84%
For some reason, it's
not in the same table.

01:20:06.050 --> 01:20:08.140 align:middle line:84%
And the other thing they just
do is put in ownership fixed

01:20:08.140 --> 01:20:09.515 align:middle line:84%
effects and see
whether the owner

01:20:09.515 --> 01:20:13.180 align:middle line:84%
fixed effects increase the
r squared of the regression.

01:20:13.180 --> 01:20:15.730 align:middle line:84%
And if you put the
owner fixed effects in,

01:20:15.730 --> 01:20:18.650 align:middle line:84%
it does make a big increase in
the r squared of the regression.

01:20:18.650 --> 01:20:22.177 align:middle line:84%
It more than doubles the r
squared from 0.18 to 0.44.

01:20:22.177 --> 01:20:24.760 align:middle line:84%
But obviously, you're putting
in an awful lot of fixed effects

01:20:24.760 --> 01:20:26.000 align:middle line:90%
on the right side.

01:20:26.000 --> 01:20:30.520 align:middle line:84%
And so what they note is that if
you put in state fixed effects

01:20:30.520 --> 01:20:33.310 align:middle line:84%
instead of owner fixed effects
and then put in state fixed

01:20:33.310 --> 01:20:36.280 align:middle line:84%
effects and then an
ownership effect,

01:20:36.280 --> 01:20:39.490 align:middle line:84%
then the ownership effect
is not significant.

01:20:39.490 --> 01:20:44.080 align:middle line:84%
So while the ownership fixed
effects do increase the fit,

01:20:44.080 --> 01:20:46.637 align:middle line:84%
you put in owner fixed effects
and state fixed effects,

01:20:46.637 --> 01:20:49.220 align:middle line:84%
suddenly you can't tell whether
the owner effects are actually

01:20:49.220 --> 01:20:50.900 align:middle line:90%
there or not at all.

01:20:50.900 --> 01:20:54.740 align:middle line:84%
So they take this
to argue that it

01:20:54.740 --> 01:20:57.500 align:middle line:84%
looks like there isn't much
evidence that owner slant--

01:20:57.500 --> 01:21:01.110 align:middle line:84%
it looks like the "Rupert
Murdoch is just making money"

01:21:01.110 --> 01:21:05.030 align:middle line:84%
story works pretty well here,
and it's hard to say that

01:21:05.030 --> 01:21:07.690 align:middle line:84%
there's a lot of media
bias other than that.

01:21:07.690 --> 01:21:09.440 align:middle line:84%
They also put in--
they do a version where

01:21:09.440 --> 01:21:14.810 align:middle line:84%
they put in owners' political
contributions on the right side.

01:21:14.810 --> 01:21:17.840 align:middle line:84%
And it's like, well, this share
Republican in the ZIP code

01:21:17.840 --> 01:21:19.640 align:middle line:84%
explains 20% of
the variance, it's

01:21:19.640 --> 01:21:22.220 align:middle line:84%
like the ownership political
contributions explain

01:21:22.220 --> 01:21:23.675 align:middle line:90%
5% of the variance.

01:21:23.675 --> 01:21:26.300 align:middle line:84%
So it looks like the ownership
political contributions are just

01:21:26.300 --> 01:21:29.240 align:middle line:84%
not a powerful explanatory
variable compared

01:21:29.240 --> 01:21:33.100 align:middle line:84%
to the Republican-ness of the
ZIP code that they're serving.

01:21:33.100 --> 01:21:51.000 align:middle line:90%